const BOOK_MC = [
// ---------- Chapter 1: Introduction ----------
["b01","According to Table 1.1, how did Scrum development at Genomica compare with the earlier waterfall approach?",["About one-tenth of the effort and seven times the velocity","About half of the effort and roughly twice the earlier velocity","The same effort, but three times the velocity","Twice the effort, but ten times the velocity"],"Tabella 1.1: effort 10x con il waterfall contro 1x con Scrum, velocity 1x contro 7x, soddisfazione del cliente da scarsa a eccellente."],
["b01","Why did Genomica's earlier attempt at a next-generation platform become a cautionary tale?",["After a year of architecture-only work, moving between two fields of the first real application took 42 seconds","The architecture was delivered on time, but no customer was willing to pay for the new platform","The team skipped architecture entirely and had to rewrite the core of the product twice in one year","The platform worked well, but the partner changed the interface of its DNA sequencers at the very last minute"],"Quasi un anno di sola architettura: alla prima applicazione reale servivano 42 secondi per passare da un campo all'altro. Da qui la scelta di un po' di design up-front unito a molto design emergente, just-in-time."],
["b01","Who published the first paper on Scrum, and where?",["Ken Schwaber, at OOPSLA in 1995","Jeff Sutherland, at Easel in 1993","Mike Cohn, at Agile 2001 in Snowbird","Kent Beck, at the XP 2000 conference"],"1986: articolo di Takeuchi e Nonaka. 1993: Sutherland e il suo team a Easel creano il processo Scrum. 1995: Schwaber pubblica il primo paper su Scrum a OOPSLA."],
["b01","In 1993 Jeff Sutherland's team at Easel created Scrum by combining the 1986 article with which other concepts?",["Object-oriented development, empirical process control, iterative and incremental development, complex adaptive systems","Rational Unified Process phases, use-case modeling, formal change control boards and earned value management","Lean manufacturing, Six Sigma statistical process control, the PMBOK project lifecycle and the CMM capability maturity levels","Extreme Programming practices, user stories, Planning Poker estimation and continuous deployment pipelines"],"Rubin elenca: sviluppo object-oriented, controllo empirico dei processi, sviluppo iterativo e incrementale, ricerca su processi software e produttività, sistemi adattivi complessi."],
["b01","In the Cynefin framework, which response pattern belongs to the complex domain?",["Probe, sense, respond","Sense, analyze, respond","Sense, categorize, respond","Act, sense, respond"],"Complesso: probe, sense, respond (esplorare, ispezionare, adattare), il terreno ideale di Scrum. Complicato: sense, analyze, respond. Semplice: sense, categorize, respond. Caotico: act, sense, respond."],
["b01","Which Cynefin domain is \"the domain of good practices dominated by experts\", where much day-to-day software maintenance falls?",["Complicated","Simple","Complex","Chaotic"],"Complicato: più risposte giuste, ma serve la diagnosi di esperti (per esempio ottimizzazione delle prestazioni, manutenzione quotidiana). Scrum può funzionare ma non è per forza la scelta migliore."],
["b01","You realize you are in the Cynefin disorder domain. What does Rubin recommend?",["Break the situation into parts and assign each part to one of the other four domains","Apply Scrum, since it is designed for situations where cause and effect are unknown","Fall back to a phase-based sequential process until the situation becomes clearer","Let one person take charge and act at once to stop further damage to the business"],"Nel disordine non sai in che dominio sei e tendi ad agire secondo le tue preferenze. Non si applica Scrum: si esce dal disordine scomponendo la situazione e assegnando ogni parte a un altro dominio."],
["b01","Why does Rubin say Scrum is a poor fit for a customer-support group?",["Requests arrive continuously and priorities can change hourly, so plans of a week or more are unreliable","Support work needs no prioritization, so a product backlog would have nothing useful to order","Support engineers are specialists, so they cannot be organized into cross-functional teams","Support requests are too small and too similar to each other to be estimated with story points or ideal days"],"Lavoro guidato dalle interruzioni: non si riesce a pianificare iterazioni di una settimana o più. Rubin suggerisce Kanban, il cui punto di forza sono manutenzione e supporto."],
["b01","A published study claims your product's algorithm is flawed, customers are suing, and the lead designer is unreachable. In Cynefin terms, what is needed?",["Someone takes charge and acts immediately to stem the bleeding","A prioritized backlog of fixes worked in the next one-week sprint","An expert analysis of several options before any action is taken","The predefined procedure that the company uses for known incidents"],"È il dominio caotico: crisi, serve una risposta rapida per ristabilire un minimo di ordine. Non è il momento di ordinare un backlog e pianificare la prossima iterazione."],
["b01","Which statement reflects Rubin's view of software development in Cynefin terms?",["Its activities span several domains, so calling all of it complex would be naive","It is always complex, so Scrum is always the most efficient choice for it","It is always complicated, so experts should analyze all of it up front","It is mostly simple, so repeatable procedures fit nearly all of its work"],"Lo sviluppo software ha attività in tutti i domini: la maggior parte cade in complicato o complesso, ma la manutenzione quotidiana è complicata e una crisi è caotica. Dire che è sempre complesso, sempre complicato o quasi tutto semplice ignora questa varietà: bisogna capire la situazione prima di scegliere l'approccio."],
["b01","Why was Scrum a good fit for Genomica, according to Rubin?",["It worked in a complex domain where more was unknown than known","It had stable requirements and needed a more predictable schedule","It needed a process certified for regulated, document-driven work","Its teams were organized by function, which is what Scrum requires"],"Genomica costruiva prodotti mai fatti prima, doveva esplorare in fretta e mostrare risultati funzionanti a un partner ogni poche settimane. Voleva anche team cross-funzionali e meno architettura up-front."],
// ---------- Chapter 2: Scrum Framework ----------
["b02","Why is Scrum described as a framework rather than a complete methodology?",["It defines a minimal set of roles, activities and artifacts, and teams add the practices their context needs","It defines a complete phase-by-phase process covering everything from requirements to deployment","It defines roles and activities, but each team invents its own artifacts and the rules for using them","It defines roles and artifacts, plus engineering practices such as test-driven development that all teams adopt"],"Scrum è volutamente incompleto: dà una struttura minima di ruoli, attività e artefatti e lascia ai team le pratiche adatte al contesto. Non è un processo a fasi completo, gli artefatti li definisce (product backlog, sprint backlog, incremento) e non impone pratiche di ingegneria come il TDD."],
["b02","Which statement correctly contrasts the sprint review with the sprint retrospective?",["The review inspects and adapts the product; the retrospective inspects and adapts the process","Both inspect the product, but only the retrospective includes the stakeholders","The review inspects the process; the retrospective inspects the product increment","The review plans the next sprint; the retrospective approves the increment for shipping to customers"],"Review: si guarda il prodotto con gli stakeholder. Retrospective: lo Scrum team guarda il proprio processo di lavoro."],
["b02","According to Chapter 2, how long does sprint planning usually take for sprints of two weeks to a month?",["About four to eight hours","About fifteen minutes","About two full days","About one hour in total"],"Rubin: per sprint da due settimane a un mese, circa quattro-otto ore. Uno sprint di una settimana non dovrebbe richiedere più di un paio d'ore."],
["b02","Which three questions does a common daily scrum format ask each team member?",["What did I accomplish, what will I work on next, and what impediments are blocking me?","What did the team deliver, what is our velocity, and who is behind the schedule?","Which tasks were assigned to me, how many hours did I log, and what is my estimate?","What did the customer ask for, what did I promise, and what will I show at the review?"],"Le tre domande riguardano il singolo: cosa ho fatto dall'ultimo daily scrum, su cosa lavorerò fino al prossimo, quali ostacoli mi bloccano. Velocity, ritardi da segnalare a un capo, ore registrate o promesse al cliente trasformerebbero il daily scrum in una riunione di stato."],
["b02","What does Rubin say about the daily scrum and problem solving?",["It is not a problem-solving activity: problems are discussed afterwards by the people involved","It is the main place where the whole team solves technical problems together every day","Problems are noted and the ScrumMaster solves them alone before the next daily scrum","Problems are collected and solved only at the sprint retrospective, at the end of the sprint"],"Il daily scrum non è problem solving e non è una riunione di stato per un capo: è ispezione, sincronizzazione e pianificazione adattiva. I problemi si discutono dopo, in piccolo gruppo."],
["b02","How does Rubin apply the pigs and chickens metaphor?",["Everyone on the Scrum team, product owner included, is a pig; people outside it are chickens","Only development team members are pigs; the product owner and ScrumMaster are chickens","Stakeholders are pigs because they fund the work; the Scrum team members are chickens","Pigs speak at the sprint review, while chickens may speak only at the daily scrum"],"Rubin considera pig (committed) tutto lo Scrum team, anche il product owner che non è obbligato al daily scrum. Chi non è nello Scrum team è un chicken e al daily scrum osserva."],
["b02","What does \"potentially shippable\" mean in Rubin's book?",["A state of confidence that the work is really done, so it could ship if the business decides to","That the increment must be shipped to the customers at the end of every single sprint","That most features are complete and the remaining testing will be done later, in a dedicated release sprint","That the product owner has formally signed off the increment for next quarter's release"],"Potenzialmente rilasciabile non vuol dire rilasciato: rilasciare è una decisione di business. Vuol dire che non resta lavoro importante non fatto (test, integrazione)."],
["b02","Why does the product owner need a size estimate for product backlog items?",["Size equates to cost, which is needed to set an item's priority","Size decides which developer will be assigned to work on the item","Size is used to compare the productivity of the different teams","Size tells each developer how many hours to log on the item"],"Scrum non impone un'unità di misura, ma il PO deve conoscere il costo di un elemento per decidere la sua priorità. Molti team usano story point o ideal day."],
["b02","In what order do the activities at the end of a sprint take place?",["Sprint review, then sprint retrospective, then the next sprint planning","Sprint retrospective, then sprint review, then the next sprint planning","Sprint review, then the next sprint planning, then sprint retrospective","Sprint retrospective, then a grooming workshop, then sprint review"],"La review è la penultima attività dello sprint, la retrospective l'ultima. Poi il ciclo riparte con lo sprint planning successivo."],
// ---------- Chapter 3: Agile Principles ----------
["b03","A team knows exactly which features customers want but is unsure which architecture and technology will deliver them. What is it mainly facing?",["Means uncertainty: uncertainty about how to build it","Variability: differences in how long similar tasks take","End uncertainty: uncertainty about what to build","Customer uncertainty: uncertainty about who will buy it"],"Sapere cosa costruire ma non come (architettura, tecnologie) è means uncertainty, l'incertezza sul come. End uncertainty riguarda il cosa, customer uncertainty il chi; la variabilità è la differenza di durata tra lavori simili, non un tipo di incertezza di Laufer."],
["b03","Which situation most strongly favors an adaptive (inspect-and-adapt) approach over a predictive (plan-then-execute) one?",["Both what to build and how to build it are partly unknown, and learning will change the plan","The requirements are known and stable, but the delivery schedule spans several years","The team is large, geographically distributed and needs a lot of coordination","The work is a repeatable activity where earlier, similar projects give accurate and stable estimates"],"L'approccio adattivo conviene quando non si sa bene né cosa costruire né come, e quello che si impara cambierà il piano. Requisiti stabili con una scadenza lunga o lavoro ripetibile con stime affidabili si prestano alla previsione; la dimensione del team non decide tra i due approcci."],
["b03","What is \"customer uncertainty\" (who uncertainty)?",["Not knowing who the actual customers of the product will be","Not knowing which features the final product will have","Not knowing which technologies will be used to build it","Not knowing when the product will finally be released"],"Laufer distingue end uncertainty (cosa) e means uncertainty (come). Per start-up e prodotti nuovi c'è anche la customer uncertainty (chi): si rischia un prodotto brillante per il mercato sbagliato."],
["b03","How do plan-driven development and Scrum differ in managing uncertainty?",["Plan-driven removes end uncertainty first and then means uncertainty; Scrum reduces all of them together","Plan-driven reduces all uncertainties together; Scrum removes end uncertainty first and then means","Plan-driven ignores means uncertainty entirely, while Scrum ignores end uncertainty until the first release","Both remove end uncertainty first, but Scrum does it with shorter phases and smaller documents"],"Il plan-driven definisce prima tutto il cosa e solo dopo affronta il come. Scrum riduce contemporaneamente tutte le incertezze (end, means, customer), con iterazioni e feedback."],
["b03","When is the \"last responsible moment\" to make an important, irreversible decision?",["When the cost of not deciding becomes greater than the cost of deciding","When every piece of information about the decision has been collected","At the end of the phase in which the plan scheduled that decision","When the product owner can no longer postpone the next sprint review"],"Principio keep options open (Poppendieck): si rimandano le decisioni importanti e irreversibili fino al momento in cui non decidere costa più che decidere."],
["b03","\"Watch the baton, not the runners\" illustrates which principle?",["Focus on idle work, not idle workers","Go fast but never hurry","Use economically sensible batch sizes","Measure progress by validating working assets"],"Nella staffetta non si vince tenendo i corridori occupati al 100%, ma portando il testimone al traguardo. Il lavoro fermo costa più delle persone ferme."],
["b03","According to Scrum's principles, how should progress be measured?",["By validating working assets that deliver value","By the phases completed and the documents approved","By conformance to the baseline plan and Gantt chart","By the share of team members who are fully utilized"],"Scrum misura l'avanzamento con asset funzionanti e validati che danno valore. Fasi superate e documenti approvati, aderenza al Gantt o utilizzo delle persone sono le misure del plan-driven, che dicono quanto lavoro si è fatto, non quanto valore si è ottenuto."],
["b03","What does \"employ minimally sufficient ceremony\" mean?",["Drop formality that adds no value, but keep documents with real economic value","Drop all documentation, since working software is the only measure of progress","Keep the same high formality on every project to ensure full traceability","Hold the Scrum activities only when the team feels they are needed"],"Scrum non è contro la documentazione: si evita il «process for the sake of process». In un contesto regolato (un pacemaker) l'asticella della cerimonia minima è più alta."],
["b03","Why does Scrum question the economies-of-scale belief in product development?",["Large batches delay feedback and increase the waste when something changes","Large batches are cheaper per unit, so Scrum makes every batch as large as possible","Scrum requires a batch size of exactly one item in every situation","Batch size only matters in manufacturing, not in software development"],"Il plan-driven lavora «all before any» (batch del 100%). Scrum usa batch piccoli ed economicamente sensati; il batch di uno può andare bene, ma non è un obiettivo in sé."],
["b03","How does the cost of change curve with Scrum compare with sequential development?",["With Scrum it starts rising steeply much later, because work in process is kept low","With Scrum it stays constant over time, whatever the size of the requested change","With Scrum it is higher, because the design is never fixed during an up-front phase","It is identical, because the cost of change depends only on the technology used"],"Nel sequenziale l'inventario cresce e il punto di flesso arriva presto. Gestendo WIP e flusso, in Scrum il costo del cambiamento dipende meno dal tempo e il flesso arriva più tardi."],
// ---------- Chapter 4: Sprints ----------
["b04","Which factor most directly argues for choosing a shorter sprint length?",["High uncertainty about what to build, so frequent feedback is valuable","A stable, well-understood product with little expected change","A rule that sprint length must match the length of the release","A desire to hold fewer planning, review and retrospective meetings overall"],"Molta incertezza su cosa costruire rende prezioso il feedback frequente, quindi sprint più corti. Un prodotto stabile e il desiderio di meno riunioni spingono verso sprint più lunghi, e non esiste una regola che leghi la durata dello sprint a quella della release."],
["b04","Who has the authority to abnormally terminate a sprint?",["The product owner","The ScrumMaster","The development team","Senior management"],"Il product owner, spesso su consiglio del team, quando un evento economicamente importante rende il goal non più valido. Rubin dice che succede di rado: è l'ultima risorsa."],
["b04","Midway through a sprint a stakeholder asks for a new feature. Which response best fits Scrum?",["Route it to the product owner, who adds it to the product backlog for a later sprint","Add it to the sprint and quietly drop the lowest-value item already in progress","Let the stakeholder ask the developers directly, who add it when they have spare time","Ask the ScrumMaster to extend the sprint by a few days so that the extra item fits"],"Nessun cambiamento che altera il goal durante lo sprint: le nuove richieste passano dal PO e vanno nel product backlog. La terminazione si usa solo se il goal è diventato obsoleto."],
["b04","Which list contains only benefits of timeboxing described in Chapter 4?",["Establishes a WIP limit, forces prioritization, demonstrates progress, motivates closure","Removes the need for estimates, fixes the scope, guarantees quality, shortens meetings","Allows sprint extensions, lets the goal change, maximizes utilization, avoids reviews","Fixes the release date, removes all risk, replaces the backlog, avoids retrospectives"],"Benefici del timebox: limite al WIP, obbliga a dare priorità, mostra progresso, evita il perfezionismo inutile, motiva a chiudere, migliora la prevedibilità. Le altre liste contengono cose che il timebox non fa (garantire la qualità, togliere le stime) o che vieta (estendere lo sprint, cambiare il goal)."],
["b04","What does \"bounded error\" mean as a benefit of short sprints?",["Even if we fumble a whole sprint, we have lost only a short amount of time","Estimation errors are capped at a fixed number of story points per sprint","Every defect found in a sprint must be fixed before the sprint can end","Errors in the sprint goal are corrected by the ScrumMaster during the sprint"],"Con sprint di due settimane, se sbagliamo tutto perdiamo al massimo due settimane: se si sbaglia, si sbaglia in piccolo."],
["b04","A team runs two-week sprints and has a one-day holiday in the next sprint. What does Rubin recommend?",["Keep the two-week length: the holiday only reduces the team's capacity","Extend the sprint by one day so that the capacity stays the same","Shorten that sprint to nine days and adjust the cadence of the following sprints","Cancel the sprint and restart the cadence after the holiday"],"La durata resta costante; festività e formazione riducono la capacità di quello sprint, non ne cambiano la lunghezza. La cadenza semplifica pianificazione e coordinamento."],
["b04","During a sprint, what distinguishes a clarification from a change?",["A clarification adds detail that helps meet the sprint goal; a change alters the goal or its scope","A clarification comes from the product owner; a change comes from the stakeholders","A clarification is written in the sprint backlog; a change is discussed at the daily scrum","A clarification is always free; a change is allowed if the team agrees to work overtime"],"Le domande di chiarimento sono normali e il PO deve rispondere durante lo sprint. Un cambiamento che altera il goal invece non è permesso, salvo ragioni economiche forti."],
["b04","What is the mutual commitment behind the sprint goal?",["The team commits to meeting the goal; the product owner commits to not altering it","The team commits to every task; the ScrumMaster commits to removing every impediment","The product owner commits to a scope; the team commits to a fixed number of hours","The stakeholders commit to the budget; the team commits to the release date"],"Il goal è un impegno reciproco: il team lo raggiunge entro la fine dello sprint, il product owner non lo cambia durante lo sprint."],
["b04","Several teams share a cadence. One team terminates its two-week sprint after one week. Which options does Rubin prefer for its next sprint?",["A one-week sprint to the original end date, or a three-week sprint covering both periods","A normal two-week sprint starting immediately, even if the team leaves the shared cadence","No sprint for one week, then the usual two-week sprints from the original calendar","A sprint of any length the team prefers, since cadence no longer matters after termination"],"Opzione 1: stessa durata (ma il team si desincronizza). Opzione 2: sprint corto fino alla data originale. Opzione 3: sprint lungo che copre i due periodi. Con più team si preferiscono la 2 o la 3."],
["b04","On the last day of the sprint, a product backlog item still has a significant defect. What happens?",["It is not done: it goes back to the product backlog and is reordered","It counts as done if the defect is logged for the next sprint","The sprint is extended by a day or two to fix the defect","It is shown as done at the review, with the defect noted"],"Non è done e lo sprint non si allunga: l'elemento torna nel product backlog, ordinato rispetto agli altri, e potrà essere finito in uno sprint futuro."],
["b04","How do the definition of done and acceptance criteria relate?",["Acceptance criteria are item-specific and apply in addition to the DoD, which applies to all items","Acceptance criteria replace the DoD for the items that the product owner has already reviewed and approved","The DoD is item-specific, while acceptance criteria apply to the whole product increment","They are the same checklist, written by the team and approved by the product owner"],"Un PBI è done solo se rispetta sia i suoi criteri di accettazione (per esempio «funziona con AmEx, Visa e MasterCard») sia la DoD dello sprint (per esempio «live in produzione»)."],
["b04","What does Rubin say about using \"done\" versus \"done-done\"?",["Teams shouldn't need two concepts; done-done is often a crutch for teams not used to really finishing","Done-done is required by Scrum for items that must also pass the product owner's acceptance","Done-done is the state reached at the release, while done is the state reached at the end of each sprint","Done-done replaces the definition of done for teams that ship to production every sprint"],"Done-done serve a chi chiama «done» il lavoro che era disposto a fare. Un team che ha capito che done vuol dire tutto il lavoro necessario non ha bisogno di due concetti."],
// ---------- Chapter 5: Requirements and User Stories ----------
["b05","Which option correctly describes the three Cs of a user story?",["Card: a short reminder; Conversation: shared understanding of details; Confirmation: acceptance tests","Card: the size estimate; Conversation: the daily scrum; Confirmation: the demo at the sprint review","Card: a full specification; Conversation: a formal review; Confirmation: the customer's signature","Card: a short reminder; Conversation: a written change request; Confirmation: manager approval"],"Le tre C di Ron Jeffries: la card è un promemoria, la conversation costruisce la comprensione condivisa, la confirmation sono le condizioni di soddisfazione e i test."],
["b05","Why does Scrum favor just-enough, just-in-time requirements?",["Early detail is likely to be wasted as needs and knowledge change","Detailed requirements are useful only to the person who wrote them","Teams cannot estimate stories that have any detail written down","Scrum forbids written requirements and relies on conversation only"],"Il dettaglio scritto molto prima dell'implementazione rischia di diventare obsoleto. Scrum non vieta di scrivere: rimanda il dettaglio a quando serve."],
["b05","Which sizes does Rubin's user story abstraction hierarchy associate with each level?",["Epics: months; features: weeks; sprintable stories: days; tasks: hours","Epics: weeks; features: days; sprintable stories: hours; tasks: minutes","Epics: a release; features: a sprint; stories: a day; tasks: an hour each","Epics: days; features: weeks; sprintable stories: months; tasks: sprints"],"Figura 5.5: mesi (più grande di una release), settimane (più grande di uno sprint), giorni (pronte per lo sprint), ore (task)."],
["b05","Who proposed the INVEST criteria, and who described the three Cs?",["Bill Wake proposed INVEST; Ron Jeffries described the three Cs","Ron Jeffries proposed INVEST; Bill Wake described the three Cs","Mike Cohn proposed INVEST; Jeff Patton described the three Cs","Jeff Patton proposed INVEST; Mike Cohn described the three Cs"],"INVEST è di Bill Wake (2003), le tre C di Ron Jeffries (2001). Patton è l'autore dello story mapping, Cohn del formato «As a... I want... so that»."],
["b05","Which INVEST criterion must every story in the product backlog satisfy, even when others are relaxed?",["Valuable","Independent","Negotiable","Small"],"Rubin: non tutte le storie sono indipendenti e non tutte sono del tutto negoziabili, ma devono essere tutte di valore dal punto di vista del product owner."],
["b05","According to Rubin, how should most technical stories (work of value only to developers) be handled?",["As tasks for getting business-valuable stories done, implied by a strong definition of done","As separate high-priority stories that the team schedules without asking the product owner","As epics in a dedicated technical backlog that is owned and ordered by the ScrumMaster","As stories collected for a hardening sprint that is held at the end of each release"],"Una storia tecnica sta nel product backlog solo se il PO ne capisce il valore (per esempio la migrazione a una versione supportata del DBMS). La maggior parte sono task delle storie di business."],
["b05","How does Rubin recommend handling nonfunctional requirements?",["As system-level constraints, including as many as possible in the definition of done","In a separate document that is reviewed only before the final release of the product","As epics implemented together in a dedicated performance and security sprint","As the operations team's concern, since they do not affect the product backlog"],"I requisiti non funzionali (per esempio i browser supportati) influenzano design e test di quasi tutte le storie: metterli nella DoD dà feedback rapido invece di scoprirli a fine sviluppo."],
["b05","A prototype story costs $10K. Choosing the wrong architecture would cost $500K, and a coin-flip guess is right half the time. What is the economic decision?",["Build the prototype: the information is worth about $250K, far more than its cost","Skip the prototype: exploration is never justified without a working feature","Skip it and guess: the expected cost of a wrong guess is only about $5K","Build it only if the product owner can move it to the next release"],"Valore dell'informazione: 50% di 500K = 250K, contro 10K di costo. Se sbagliare costasse 15K, l'informazione varrebbe 7,5K e converrebbe tirare a indovinare (fail fast)."],
["b05","Who usually takes part in a user-story-writing workshop, and how long does it last?",["Product owner, ScrumMaster, team and stakeholders; a few hours to a few days","Only the product owner and the business analysts; about two weeks","Only the development team; one hour per week of sprint length","The stakeholders without the team; a full month of meetings before the release starts"],"Il workshop è collettivo e mirato (per esempio in vista del release planning), non produce una specifica completa. Spesso parte da un'analisi dei ruoli utente e dalle personas."],
["b05","How are stories arranged in a story map (Jeff Patton)?",["Themes follow the user workflow left to right; stories under each theme are ordered by priority top to bottom","Stories are ordered left to right by size and top to bottom by the team that will build each of them","Epics are listed top to bottom by date, and their stories left to right by the total number of acceptance criteria","Themes are grouped by software component, and stories are ordered by the developer who estimated them"],"In alto le epic (attività dell'utente), poi i theme lungo la sequenza d'uso nel tempo, sotto ogni theme le storie in ordine di desiderabilità. Non tutte finiscono nella stessa release."],
// ---------- Chapter 6: Product Backlog ----------
["b06","In the DEEP characteristics of a healthy product backlog, what does \"Detailed appropriately\" imply?",["Items likely to be worked on soon are more detailed than items far in the future","Every item has the same level of detail, so it can be reordered at any time","Detail is added only by the product owner and never changes once it is written","Every item must have acceptance criteria and sprint tasks before it is added"],"Il dettaglio si aggiunge progressivamente: in alto elementi piccoli e dettagliati, più in basso elementi grandi e grezzi, raffinati just in time. Dettagliare tutto allo stesso modo o esigere task e criteri prima di inserire un elemento sarebbe uno spreco, e il dettaglio cambia e lo aggiungono anche team e stakeholder."],
["b06","What does it mean for a product backlog item to be \"ready\"?",["The item is clear, small and testable enough to be completed within one sprint","The item sits in the top ten of the backlog, regardless of how much detail it has","The product owner has approved the item for release to the customers","The item has been implemented and is waiting for product owner acceptance"],"Ready descrive lo stato prima dello sprint: l'elemento è chiaro, abbastanza piccolo e testabile da poter essere finito in uno sprint. Stare in cima al backlog non basta senza dettagli, l'approvazione per il rilascio arriva dopo, e un elemento implementato in attesa di accettazione riguarda il done."],
["b06","What does the DEEP acronym stand for?",["Detailed appropriately, Emergent, Estimated, Prioritized","Documented, Emergent, Elaborated, Prioritized appropriately","Detailed, Expected, Effortless, Predictable","Done, Emergent, Estimated, Potentially shippable"],"DEEP (Pichler e Cohn): Detailed appropriately, Emergent, Estimated, Prioritized. Le altre opzioni sostituiscono parole plausibili (Documented, Elaborated, Done, Potentially shippable): attenzione soprattutto a «Detailed appropriately», che non vuol dire tutto dettagliato."],
["b06","As a general rule, how much of its time should the development team give to grooming?",["Up to 10% of its time each sprint","About 25% of its time each sprint","One whole sprint in every release","None: grooming is the product owner's job"],"Il team dedica fino al 10% del tempo di ogni sprint ad aiutare il PO: creare e rivedere elementi, scomporre quelli grandi, stimarli. Il 25% toglierebbe troppo allo sprint, uno sprint dedicato a ogni release rimanderebbe il grooming, e il PO non può farlo da solo perché le stime sono del team."],
["b06","Who does product backlog grooming?",["The product owner leads it, working with the team, the ScrumMaster and stakeholders","The product owner alone, so that the team can stay focused on sprint execution","The ScrumMaster, who collects the requests and estimates them for the team","The stakeholders, who vote on the priorities at the end of every sprint"],"Il grooming è collaborativo (figura 6.7): il PO è responsabile, team e stakeholder interni ed esterni contribuiscono, lo ScrumMaster facilita. Riduce i passaggi di consegne tra business e tecnica."],
["b06","A team usually completes about 5 PBIs per sprint. Using Rubin's heuristic, how many ready items should it keep groomed?",["About 10 to 15, two to three sprints' worth","About 5, exactly one sprint's worth","All the items in the release, groomed up front","About 50, ten sprints' worth"],"Euristica: due-tre sprint di storie pronte, così la pipeline non resta mai a secco ma l'inventario non diventa eccessivo."],
["b06","In release flow management, how is the product backlog partitioned by two lines?",["Into must-have, nice-to-have and won't-have items for the release","Into ready, in-progress and done items for the current and the next sprint","Into features, defects and technical work for each team","Into epics, themes and tasks for each group of stakeholders"],"Must-have: senza questi non c'è una release vendibile. Nice-to-have: si possono sacrificare se manca tempo. Won't-have: fuori da questa release."],
["b06","What is Rubin's starting rule for how many product backlogs to have?",["One product, one product backlog","One team, one product backlog","One release, one product backlog","One stakeholder, one product backlog"],"La regola di partenza è un prodotto, un product backlog: così tutto il lavoro sul prodotto si ordina in un'unica lista dal punto di vista del prodotto intero. Un backlog per team, per release o per stakeholder spezzerebbe le priorità e farebbe competere liste separate."],
["b06","How do organizations usually handle the backlog of a very large product, such as a phone built by hundreds of teams?",["Hierarchical backlogs: one product backlog of large features, then feature-area backlogs","One flat backlog that contains every item from every team, in a single ordered list for all","One independent backlog per team, with no backlog at the product level","One backlog per release, merged by the project manager at each milestone"],"In cima c'è comunque un product backlog con le feature grandi e un chief product owner; sotto, un backlog per ogni area (per esempio audiovideo) condiviso dai team di quell'area."],
["b06","Teams working on one product are not interchangeable. What does Rubin suggest?",["Keep one backlog and give each team a view of the items that match its skills","Create a separate product backlog for each team, each with its own owner","Give every item to the team with the most free capacity, whatever its skills","Let each team take only the top items of the backlog, without exceptions"],"Si mantiene un solo backlog con viste per team. Proprio per questa rigidità molte organizzazioni puntano su code ownership condivisa e team più intercambiabili."],
["b06","One team has to work on three products. What is the first and often best solution?",["Work on one product at a time, taking items from a single backlog each sprint","Split every sprint into three equal time slices, one for each of the three products","Assign one developer to each product so that no product ever waits","Keep three backlogs and let each developer choose freely among them"],"Prima scelta: un prodotto per sprint. Se l'organizzazione impone il lavoro in parallelo, si possono fondere i backlog con un'unica prioritizzazione concordata dai product owner."],
// ---------- Chapter 7: Estimation and Velocity ----------
["b07","Which statement best describes story points?",["A relative measure of an item's bigness, reflecting complexity, size and effort","A normalized count of the ideal hours a developer will spend on the item","A measure of the business value that an item delivers to its users","A measure of how productive each individual developer is over time"],"Gli story point misurano la grandezza relativa (complessità e dimensione fisica) e devono riflettere lo sforzo dal punto di vista del team. Non misurano valore né tempo."],
["b07","260 story points remain. The last five sprints had velocities of 18, 22, 20, 24 and 21. Using the highest velocity for the best case and the lowest for the worst case, and rounding up to whole sprints, what range of sprints is needed?",["11 to 15 sprints","10 to 14 sprints","12 to 13 sprints","13 to 20 sprints"],"Caso migliore: 260 / 24 = 10,8, quindi 11 sprint. Caso peggiore: 260 / 18 = 14,4, quindi 15 sprint. 10-14 arrotonda per difetto, 12-13 usa velocity diverse dagli estremi, 13-20 non corrisponde ai dati. Il range comunica l'incertezza reale."],
["b07","Many teams size items with a modified Fibonacci sequence (1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 20, 40, 100). What is the main reason?",["Wider gaps at the large end avoid false precision on big, uncertain items","Each value of the sequence maps directly to a fixed number of ideal days","The sequence guarantees that the sizes of the parts add up to the whole","Scrum requires every team to estimate with this exact sequence"],"Più l'elemento è grande, meno precisa è la stima: i valori distanti evitano discussioni inutili tra 37 e 38 e si raggruppano elementi simili nello stesso «bin». I valori non corrispondono a giorni ideali, le parti non sommano per forza al tutto e Scrum non impone la scala."],
["b07","Which units does Rubin associate with each level of estimation?",["Portfolio: T-shirt sizes; PBIs: story points or ideal days; tasks: ideal hours","Portfolio: story points; PBIs: ideal hours; tasks: T-shirt sizes","Portfolio: ideal days; PBIs: T-shirt sizes; tasks: story points","Portfolio: dollars in exact amounts; PBIs: calendar days; tasks: elapsed hours of work"],"Tre livelli: portfolio (taglie a T, stime grezze), product backlog (story point o ideal day), task dello sprint (ideal hour, cioè ore di sforzo, non ore trascorse)."],
["b07","What is the product owner's role while the team estimates product backlog items?",["Describe the items and answer questions, without anchoring the team to an estimate","Provide the estimates, which the team then reviews and adjusts only if it disagrees","Approve the final estimate whenever the team cannot reach a consensus","Estimate the business value, while the ScrumMaster estimates the size"],"Stima il team che farà il lavoro. Il PO descrive gli elementi e risponde alle domande senza guidare verso una stima desiderata (anchoring). Non fornisce le stime né decide in caso di disaccordo, e lo ScrumMaster non stima: fa da coach e facilitatore."],
["b07","What is the main risk Rubin sees in estimating with ideal days?",["People may misinterpret ideal days as calendar days","Ideal days cannot be summed into a release forecast","Scrum forbids ideal days in favor of story points","Ideal days force the team to estimate every task in hours"],"Un ideal day è un giorno di sforzo senza interruzioni, come i 60 minuti ideali di una partita di football che ne dura più di tre. Se c'è rischio di fraintendimento, meglio gli story point."],
["b07","In Planning Poker, what happens when the revealed estimates differ widely?",["The high and low estimators explain their reasoning, then everyone estimates again","The average of all the cards becomes the estimate of the item","The product owner chooses the estimate that is closest to the value the business expected","The ScrumMaster picks the most frequent card and closes the round"],"Chi ha dato la stima più alta e la più bassa spiega il perché, così emergono ipotesi e malintesi, poi si rivota fino al consenso. Fare la media, far scegliere il PO o prendere la carta più frequente salta la discussione, che è la parte utile della tecnica."],
["b07","Who first described Planning Poker, and who popularized it?",["James Grenning described it; Mike Cohn popularized it","Mike Cohn described it; Ken Schwaber popularized it","Ron Jeffries described it; Bill Wake popularized it","Jeff Sutherland described it; Kent Beck popularized it"],"Planning Poker è stato descritto da James Grenning (2002) e reso popolare da Mike Cohn (2006). Schwaber e Sutherland sono i creatori di Scrum, Jeffries e Wake sono legati a user story e INVEST, Beck a Extreme Programming."],
["b07","In a Planning Poker deck, what does the infinity card mean?",["The item is so large that it makes no sense to put a number on it","The item cannot be done because it depends on another team","The estimator needs a break before the session continues","The item has no value and should leave the product backlog"],"∞: troppo grande per essere stimato. Il ? indica che non si sa stimare, la carta pi greco o la tazza di caffè che serve una pausa."],
["b07","A new team has no historical velocity. How can it forecast its velocity, according to Rubin?",["Plan one or two sprints and sum the sizes of the items it would commit to","Use the average velocity of the organization's best-performing team","Multiply the number of team members by the sprint length in days","Avoid any release forecast until it has completed at least three sprints"],"Si fa uno sprint planning di prova e si somma la dimensione degli elementi che il team si impegnerebbe a fare; pianificandone due si ottiene un range. La velocity di un altro team non è confrontabile, un calcolo su persone e giorni ignora il lavoro reale, e aspettare tre sprint lascia senza previsioni."],
["b07","What usually happens to velocity during and after a long period of overtime?",["A short rise, then a steeper decline and a recovery period that outweigh the gain","A permanent rise, because the team learns to work faster and harder under pressure","No change, because velocity counts only items done to the DoD","A gradual rise, as long as the overtime lasts less than a month"],"Figura 7.14: lo straordinario dà un picco breve, seguito da un calo di velocity e di qualità; l'area persa nel recupero supera quella guadagnata."],
["b07","What does velocity measure?",["Output: the size of what was delivered","Outcome: the value customers obtained","Effort: the hours the team logged","Efficiency: estimated versus actual hours"],"La velocity misura l'output, cioè la dimensione di ciò che è stato consegnato, non l'outcome (il valore ottenuto dai clienti). Non misura ore lavorate né efficienza rispetto alle stime: serve per pianificare e come diagnostica interna del team."],
["b07","An executive gives the largest bonus to the team with the highest velocity. What is the likely result?",["Point inflation and cut corners, which increase technical debt","More accurate estimates, since every team wants to be precise","Higher real productivity across all of the teams involved","A common point scale that makes the teams comparable"],"Usata come misura di performance, la velocity spinge a gonfiare i punti (un 5 diventa 500) o a tagliare la qualità per finire di più. Non va usata per giudicare i team."],
// ---------- Chapter 8: Technical Debt ----------
["b08","In the technical debt metaphor, what plays the role of interest?",["The extra effort paid each time the team must work around the shortcut","The one-time cost of rewriting the shortcut code in the proper way, later on","The cost of the defects that customers find after each release","The license fees for the tools that measure code quality metrics"],"L'interesse è il costo ricorrente del convivere con la scorciatoia, pagato ogni volta che si lavora intorno al codice sbagliato. Il costo una tantum per riscriverlo bene è il capitale (principal); i difetti trovati dai clienti sono una conseguenza, non l'interesse."],
["b08","Which practice most directly prevents technical debt from entering the code base?",["A strong definition of done, with automated tests and refactoring in every sprint","Reserving the final sprint of the year for paying back all of the accumulated debt","Having a separate quality team inspect and fix the code after each sprint review","Scheduling a hardening sprint before each release to clean up the code base"],"Prevenire vuol dire costruire la qualità in ogni sprint (buone pratiche tecniche, DoD forte). Le altre opzioni rimandano o delegano la pulizia e lasciano crescere il debito."],
["b08","Which three types of technical debt does Rubin distinguish?",["Naive, unavoidable and strategic","Design, code and test","Short-term, long-term and permanent","Visible, hidden and forgotten"],"Rubin distingue debito naive (da immaturità, detto anche reckless debt o mess), inevitabile (imprevedibile, emerge imparando) e strategico (preso di proposito per ragioni economiche). Le altre terne classificano per area o durata, non per origine come fa il libro."],
["b08","Which kind of technical debt comes from team, process or organizational immaturity?",["Naive technical debt","Unavoidable technical debt","Strategic technical debt","Targeted technical debt"],"Il debito naive nasce da immaturità del team, del processo o dell'organizzazione: design approssimativo, cattiva ingegneria, poca disciplina. L'inevitabile nasce da cambiamenti non prevedibili, lo strategico è una scelta consapevole; targeted è uno stato del debito scelto per il rimborso."],
["b08","When can taking on strategic technical debt make sense?",["When a time-sensitive goal, like being first to market, is worth more than the debt","When the team wants to raise its velocity permanently, without paying any extra cost later","When the definition of done is too strict for the current sprint goal","When the product owner is not available to prioritize the clean-up work"],"Il debito strategico è uno strumento: lo si prende quando i benefici superano di molto il costo, per esempio per entrare per primi in un mercato. Va comunque reso visibile e ripagato."],
["b08","What is the \"unpredictable tipping point\" of technical debt?",["The point where debt reaches critical mass and even small changes become highly uncertain","The point where the product owner stops all feature work for a sprint to repay the debt","The point where the debt equals the original development cost of the whole product","The point where the team's velocity becomes stable again after a hardening sprint"],"Il debito cresce in modo non lineare: a un certo punto il prodotto diventa ingestibile e anche piccoli cambiamenti diventano incerti e costosi."],
["b08","Why is \"less testing accelerates velocity\" a myth?",["Skipped testing builds debt, so velocity soon falls below what testing would have allowed","Testing has no effect on velocity, because tests are not counted in story points","Less testing does accelerate velocity, but only for teams with a strong definition of done","Testing is done by a separate team, so it never affects the development team's velocity"],"Tagliare i test dà un'illusione di velocità, ma il debito accumulato fa crollare la velocity, e il debito genera altro debito (figura 8.5). Non è vero che i test non incidono sulla velocity o che li fa un altro team: in Scrum il test fa parte del lavoro dello sprint."],
["b08","Which is a way to make technical debt visible at the technical level?",["A technical debt backlog, for example cards on a board next to the sprint backlog","A dedicated section in the release notes that are delivered to the customers each time","A personal to-do list kept privately by each developer","A discussion at the yearly architecture review only"],"A livello tecnico il debito si rende visibile nel defect tracker, come elementi del product backlog o in un technical debt backlog dedicato, spesso una bacheca vicino allo sprint backlog. Release notes per i clienti, liste private o una discussione annuale non lo mettono davanti a chi pianifica lo sprint."],
["b08","What is the Boy Scout rule applied to technical debt?",["Clean up happened-upon debt when you are working in that area of the code","Schedule a clean-up sprint every time the debt backlog gets too long","Never change code owned by another team, even if it contains debt","Write a defect report for each piece of debt before changing any of the affected code"],"Lasciare il campo più pulito di come lo si è trovato: si sistema il debito scoperto mentre si lavora; se è troppo, si registra come debito noto nel backlog."],
["b08","Which technical debt should be repaid first?",["High-interest debt, such as a frequently changed module that much other code depends on","The oldest debt, whatever the frequency with which that part of the code is changed","The smallest items, so that the technical debt backlog shrinks as fast as possible","Debt in rarely used areas, since changing them carries the least risk for the product"],"Come con i debiti finanziari, si ripaga prima quello al 18% e poi quello al 6%. Il debito in codice poco usato e mai modificato costa poco interesse."],
["b08","What is Rubin's preferred way to repay known technical debt?",["Repay targeted debt while doing customer-valuable work in the same area","Dedicate a full sprint to debt reduction once in every release","Create one product backlog item for each debt item and let the PO order them","Hand the debt to a separate maintenance team after each release"],"Si evita lo sprint dedicato al debito: durante lo sprint planning si prendono le card di debito che toccano le aree delle nuove feature. Così il debito si ripaga dove serve davvero."],
["b08","Who first wrote about the technical debt metaphor?",["Ward Cunningham","Martin Fowler","Steve McConnell","Robert C. Martin"],"Ward Cunningham (1992). Fowler, McConnell e Martin hanno dato altri nomi al debito naive: reckless debt, unintentional debt, mess."],
// ---------- Chapter 9: Product Owner ----------
["b09","Which of these decisions belongs to the product owner rather than to the development team?",["Which items will deliver the most value and should be delivered first","How many product backlog items the team can take into the sprint","Which technical design and architecture best support each of the features","Which team member will work on each task during the sprint"],"Il PO decide quali elementi danno più valore e in che ordine farli. Quanto lavoro prendere nello sprint, come progettarlo e chi fa cosa sono decisioni del team che si auto-organizza."],
["b09","On a large product with many teams, how is the product owner role best scaled?",["A chief product owner with a team of product owners, each working with one or a few teams","One single product owner who personally attends the planning and daily scrum of every team","A committee of stakeholders who vote on priorities at the start of each sprint","Each development team nominates one of its own members to act as product owner"],"Ruolo gerarchico (figura 9.13): il chief product owner tiene la visione e l'ordine complessivo, i product owner dei team restano autorizzati a prendere la maggior parte delle decisioni."],
["b09","A product owner is available to the team for only an hour a week. What is the most likely consequence?",["Questions go unanswered, so the team waits or guesses and may build the wrong thing","The ScrumMaster automatically inherits the product owner's decision rights","The team is affected only if the product owner also misses the sprint review at the end","Velocity rises sharply, because the team is interrupted much less often"],"Il PO è un ruolo a tempo pieno e di impegno quotidiano. Se non c'è, il feedback arriva tardi e vale meno. Lo ScrumMaster non prende le decisioni di prodotto."],
["b09","Why does Rubin say the product owner \"faces two directions\"?",["Toward stakeholders, to be their voice, and toward the team, to say what to build and in what order","Toward management, to report status, and toward the ScrumMaster, to enforce the agreed process","Toward the current release, to fix its defects, and toward the next release, to plan new features","Toward customers, to sell the product, and toward competitors, to track their product moves"],"Verso gli stakeholder il PO fa da product manager; verso il team è in parte business analyst e in parte tester (criteri di accettazione)."],
["b09","At sprint planning, which question should a product owner ask about sprint-level economics?",["Would I write a check from my own bank account, equal to this sprint's cost, for these features?","Can the team finish more story points than last sprint, so that its velocity keeps rising?","Is every developer fully utilized on tasks that match their own specialty in this sprint?","Have the stakeholders approved the hours each developer will log during the sprint?"],"Un buon PO tratta i soldi dell'organizzazione come se fossero suoi: se non pagherebbe di tasca propria lo sprint per quelle feature, non dovrebbe farlo nemmeno con i soldi dell'azienda."],
["b09","What is the product owner's role in estimating product backlog items?",["The PO does not estimate, but is available to clarify items during estimation","The PO estimates the items, and the team then validates the numbers","The PO estimates both the value and the size, and the team estimates only the tasks","The PO and the ScrumMaster estimate together before sprint planning"],"Il PO supervisiona il grooming (creare, stimare, ordinare) ma non fa tutto da solo: le stime le fa il team, il PO risponde alle domande."],
["b09","When should the product owner verify that items meet their acceptance criteria?",["During sprint execution, as items become available, so the team knows what is done before the review","Only at the sprint review, in front of all the stakeholders, so as to formally accept or reject the work","After the release, once the users have run their own acceptance tests on the delivered product","At the next sprint planning, before the team commits to the work of the following sprint"],"Il PO definisce i criteri di accettazione e li verifica durante lo sprint: alla review è troppo tardi per correggere a basso costo."],
["b09","How should business engagement in Scrum differ from traditional development (Figure 9.4)?",["Constant high engagement, instead of the U-shaped curve with involvement only at the start and the end","High engagement only at the start, to define the complete set of requirements before development","Engagement only at the end of each release, when users run their acceptance tests on the product","The same U-shaped engagement as before, but repeated inside every sprint of the release"],"Nel tradizionale la curva è a vasca da bagno: tanto all'inizio (requisiti) e alla fine (collaudo), quasi niente in mezzo. In Scrum si costruisce una feature alla volta, quindi serve presenza costante."],
["b09","Which four groups of characteristics does Rubin list for a good product owner?",["Domain skills, people skills, decision making, accountability","Technical skills, coding skills, testing skills, estimation","Leadership, formal authority, seniority, budget ownership","Process knowledge, facilitation, coaching, protection"],"Le caratteristiche del PO sono competenze di dominio, competenze relazionali, capacità di decidere e responsabilità del risultato. Competenze tecniche di codice e test servono al team, l'autorità formale non basta senza decisioni, e coaching e protezione sono tratti dello ScrumMaster."],
["b09","An internal IT group builds a system for the marketing group. Who should be the product owner (Table 9.1)?",["An empowered person from the marketing group that will benefit from the system","The IT business analyst, because the people in the marketing group are too busy for it","The IT development manager, who knows the technical constraints best","The ScrumMaster, who coordinates between marketing and the IT team"],"Sviluppo interno: un rappresentante del business che beneficia del sistema. Se è troppo impegnato, meglio liberargli tempo e usare la persona IT come proxy, non come product owner."],
["b09","Who is typically the product owner of a component team?",["A technical person who can best prioritize the requests coming from feature teams","The chief product owner of the whole product, who handles every component directly","A marketing product manager who represents the end customers of the product","The ScrumMaster of the feature teams that use the component the most"],"Tabella 9.1: commerciale, un proxy interno come il product manager; outsourcing, qualcuno dell'azienda che paga; component team, una persona tecnica."],
["b09","What is a product owner proxy?",["Someone the product owner empowers to make some tactical decisions on the product owner's behalf","A second product owner who shares the final decision rights equally with the first one","A senior stakeholder who can override the product owner's priorities whenever the business requires it","A team member who represents the product owner at the daily scrum and at the review"],"Tutti sanno che il proxy non è il product owner, ma ha delega per alcune decisioni quotidiane. Il PO non può delegare la responsabilità finale."],
["b09","Which role combination does Rubin consider a bad idea?",["Product owner and ScrumMaster on the same Scrum team","Product owner of two teams working on the same product","Product owner and developer on the same team, at times","ScrumMaster of two different Scrum teams at the same time"],"PO e ScrumMaster si bilanciano a vicenda: unirli crea un conflitto di interessi. Essere PO di più team (meglio se sullo stesso prodotto) o, a volte, anche sviluppatore è possibile."],
["b09","When is a product owner team acceptable?",["When one person is still the ultimate decision maker and it doesn't become design by committee","When each member votes and decisions pass with a simple majority of the members of the product owner team","When it includes every stakeholder who funds the product, so that each one has a voice","Always, because the product owner role is too large for any single person to perform"],"Va bene se resta un unico decisore finale. Attenzione: un PO team può nascondere un problema reale (troppi progetti avviati, prodotto troppo grande, PO senza le competenze)."],
// ---------- Chapter 10: ScrumMaster ----------
["b10","Which description best captures the ScrumMaster role?",["A servant leader and coach who helps the team and organization use Scrum well","A project manager who assigns the tasks and tracks progress against the plan","The team's most senior technical person, who makes the final design decisions","A proxy product owner who sets the priorities whenever the product owner is busy"],"Il PO cura che si costruisca il prodotto giusto, il team che lo si costruisca bene, lo ScrumMaster aiuta tutti a capire e adottare valori, principi e pratiche di Scrum, come coach e servant leader. Non assegna task come un project manager, non prende le decisioni tecniche e non sostituisce il PO."],
["b10","One team member keeps dominating the daily scrum. What would a ScrumMaster most likely do first?",["Coach the person and the team so they learn to handle it, stepping in only if needed","Report the behavior to the person's line manager for the next performance review","Take over the meeting and call on each person in turn, with a strict time limit","Ask the product owner to decide who may speak and for how long at each daily scrum"],"Lo ScrumMaster fa da coach: «non sono qui per risolvere i vostri problemi, ma per aiutarvi a risolverli», quindi aiuta la persona e il team a gestire la situazione e interviene in modo diretto solo se serve. Segnalare al manager, prendere il controllo della riunione o delegare al PO tolgono al team l'occasione di auto-organizzarsi."],
["b10","What is the best assessment of one person serving as both product owner and ScrumMaster for the same team?",["Not recommended: the roles counterbalance each other, creating a conflict of interest","Acceptable, provided that the person is also the line manager of the whole team","Recommended for small teams, because it cuts the overhead of having two roles","Required during the first three sprints, until the team learns how Scrum works"],"Lo ScrumMaster è il coach del PO, e non ci si può fare da coach da soli. Inoltre bilancia le richieste del PO con le capacità del team."],
["b10","Which list matches the ScrumMaster responsibilities of Figure 10.1?",["Coach, servant leader, process authority, interference shield, impediment remover, change agent","Coach, task assigner, process authority, status reporter for management, impediment remover, change agent","Manager, servant leader, product authority, interference shield, tester, change agent","Coach, servant leader, technical lead, budget owner, impediment remover, hiring manager"],"Sei responsabilità: coach, servant leader, autorità di processo, scudo dalle interferenze, rimozione degli impedimenti, agente del cambiamento."],
["b10","Which question would a servant leader ask?",["\"What can I do today to help you and the team be more effective?\"","\"So, what are you going to do for me today to help the project succeed?\"","\"Why is your task still not finished, and when exactly will it be done?\"","\"Who approved this change to the plan we agreed on?\""],"Il servant leader serve prima di tutto il team e chiede come può aiutarlo a essere più efficace. Le altre domande sono quelle di un capo tradizionale: chiedere cosa si fa per lui, pretendere spiegazioni sui ritardi o chiedere chi ha approvato un cambiamento."],
["b10","What does it mean that the ScrumMaster is the team's \"process authority\"?",["Ensuring the team enacts Scrum and its own approaches, without hiring, firing or dictating tasks","Hiring and firing those team members who do not follow the process that the team itself agreed on","Deciding which tasks each developer works on and in what order during the sprint","Being accountable for making sure that all the committed work actually gets done"],"L'autorità non è quella di un manager: lo ScrumMaster non assegna lavoro e non risponde del fatto che il lavoro venga fatto, ma aiuta il team a definire e seguire il proprio processo."],
["b10","A team keeps missing sprint goals because of unstable production servers used for testing, controlled only by the VP of Operations. What should the ScrumMaster do?",["Take ownership of the impediment and work with the VP of Operations to fix it","Ask the team to remove server testing from its definition of done for now","Tell the team to solve it alone, since the impediment affects their own work","Report the missed goals to management and wait for further instructions"],"È l'esempio del libro: quando il team non può rimuovere un impedimento da solo, lo ScrumMaster se ne fa carico e lavora con chi può risolverlo."],
["b10","Why do great ScrumMasters rarely answer questions directly?",["Probing questions help people find their own answers (Socratic questioning)","They usually lack the technical knowledge needed to answer them","Scrum forbids the ScrumMaster from giving advice to the team","Every answer must come from the product owner, who owns all of the team's decisions"],"Lo ScrumMaster è questioning e paziente: fa domande profonde e lascia al team il tempo di arrivare alla risposta. Credersi più intelligente del team intero sarebbe arrogante."],
["b10","Which analogy does Rubin use for the protective side of the ScrumMaster?",["A sheepdog guarding the flock from wolves","A referee enforcing the rules of the game","A captain steering the ship through storms","A conductor setting the orchestra's tempo"],"Il cane pastore protegge il gregge dai lupi (impedimenti, agende diverse) e riporta indietro chi si allontana verso le vecchie abitudini non agili."],
["b10","As a Scrum team matures, how does the ScrumMaster's work typically change?",["Less day-to-day coaching, more work on organizational impediments and change","Less work overall, so the role usually disappears after the first year","More task-level development work inside the team in every sprint","More time spent writing detailed status reports for management"],"Man mano che il team matura serve meno coaching quotidiano, ma cresce il lavoro come agente del cambiamento sugli impedimenti dell'organizzazione: il ruolo resta importante e non scompare. Lo ScrumMaster non diventa uno sviluppatore né un compilatore di report per il management."],
["b10","A ScrumMaster has spare capacity. Which option does Rubin personally prefer?",["Being ScrumMaster for more than one Scrum team","Becoming a developer on the same Scrum team","Becoming product owner of the same Scrum team","Taking over the team members' performance reviews"],"Fare anche lo sviluppatore è possibile, ma crea un conflitto quando impedimenti e task urgenti arrivano insieme. Rubin preferisce condividere le competenze di ScrumMaster con più team."],
["b10","Why might a tech lead not be the best choice as ScrumMaster?",["Time spent on ScrumMaster work is time not spent on technical leadership","Tech leads are not allowed to attend the daily scrum of their team","Scrum requires the ScrumMaster to have no technical knowledge at all about the product","Tech leads always end up becoming the managers of the team members"],"Il tech lead è lì perché è molto bravo tecnicamente: come ScrumMaster quella eccellenza non viene sfruttata e il risultato tecnico può peggiorare."],
["b10","Which list contains the ScrumMaster characteristics of Figure 10.2?",["Knowledgeable, questioning, patient, collaborative, protective, transparent","Authoritative, decisive, highly technical, demanding, protective, transparent","Knowledgeable, directive, fast, independent, strict, confidential","Senior, certified, technical, persuasive, political, highly visible"],"Figura 10.2: competente (su Scrum, con conoscenze tecniche e di dominio ragionevoli), fa domande, paziente, collaborativo, protettivo, trasparente. Autoritario, direttivo, riservato o politico descrivono un manager tradizionale, l'opposto del servant leader."],
// ---------- Chapter 11: Development Team ----------
["b11","What team size does Rubin recommend for a Scrum development team?",["Five to nine people","Two to four people","Ten to fifteen people","Twelve to twenty people"],"Rubin: la regola generale è da cinque a nove persone, e per esperienza da cinque a sette è il punto ideale. Con meno di cinque spesso mancano competenze, con più di nove crescono i canali di comunicazione: per lavori grandi si usano più team, non team più grandi."],
["b11","A manager wants to assign tasks to developers so that the sprint finishes on time. What does self-organization suggest?",["The team decides who does what; the manager provides goals, resources and boundaries","The manager should assign the tasks, since the manager is accountable for results","The ScrumMaster should assign the tasks on the manager's behalf to avoid conflicts","The product owner should assign the tasks, since the product owner knows the priorities"],"Auto-organizzazione: proprietà emergente, dal basso, come lo stormo di uccelli con poche regole semplici. I manager creano e ricreano l'ambiente in cui il team si organizza."],
["b11","Why do stable, dedicated teams generally outperform frequently reshuffled, part-time ones?",["They build trust and a shared velocity history, and avoid context switching","They may skip retrospectives, because they already know their own process","They can be loaded to 100% of their capacity without any risk of delay","Scrum forbids changing the membership of a team under any circumstances"],"La stabilità crea fiducia e prevedibilità; la dedizione evita la perdita di produttività del multitasking. Scrum non vieta i cambi, scoraggia quelli inutili."],
["b11","With N(N-1)/2 communication channels, how many channels does a team of 10 people have?",["45","10","20","90"],"Con N(N-1)/2: 10 × 9 / 2 = 45 canali. 10 è il numero di canali di un team di 5 persone, 90 dimentica di dividere per due (ogni coppia si conta una volta). I canali crescono con il quadrato delle persone: per questo i team piccoli comunicano meglio."],
["b11","What does the Musketeer attitude mean for a development team?",["\"All for one and one for all\": the team succeeds or fails together","Each member defends their own specialty against the other members","A few senior members lead, and the others follow their decisions","The team stands against the product owner to protect its scope"],"«Tutti per uno, uno per tutti»: il team riesce o fallisce insieme e nessuno è lì solo «per farsi un giro». Difendere la propria specialità, seguire pochi senior o fare muro contro il PO sono atteggiamenti da gruppo, non da team. Le competenze a T favoriscono questo atteggiamento."],
["b11","What does Rubin say about keeping a separate testing or QA team in Scrum?",["Usually unnecessary: the team should test in every sprint, unless a regulation requires one","Required, so that developers never test their own code during the sprint and testing stays independent","It is recommended for the hardening sprint that is held at the end of every release","It is allowed only when the QA team reports to the ScrumMaster of the development team"],"I team per ruolo (design, sviluppo, test) si passano il lavoro e sono un serio impedimento. Il test deve essere intrecciato al lavoro di ogni sprint."],
["b11","What are T-shaped skills?",["Deep skills in one specialty, plus the ability to work outside it","Equal, expert-level skills in every area of the team's work","Skills in one specialty only, with no work done outside it","Management skills added on top of a technical specialty"],"Competenze a T: una specialità profonda (la barra verticale) più la capacità di aiutare in altre aree, per esempio testing o documentazione, quando c'è un collo di bottiglia (la barra orizzontale). Non si pretende che tutti siano esperti di tutto, né si accettano specialisti che lavorano solo nella propria area."],
["b11","Sue, a UX designer, is needed by one team only about 10% of the time. What does Rubin suggest?",["Share Sue among a reasonable number of teams, and have Sue help others learn UX basics","Keep Sue full time on one team, so that UX skills are always available there when needed","Split Sue across as many teams as possible, in slices of 10% each","Drop UX work from the product until a full-time UX designer can be hired"],"Lo specialista si può dividere tra alcuni team, ma non tanti da farne un collo di bottiglia (la staffetta con il testimone a terra). Meglio diffondere le competenze per dipendere meno dagli specialisti."],
["b11","According to the Wheelwright and Clark data, how many concurrent projects make economic sense for one person?",["One or two: with three or more, coordination eats the value-adding time","Exactly one, because a second project halves a person's productivity","Three to five, so that there is always unblocked work to switch to","Any number, as long as the person stays fully utilized every day"],"Con due progetti si è un po' più produttivi (se uno è bloccato si passa all'altro); da tre in su si perde tempo a coordinarsi, ricordare e cercare informazioni. Un secondo progetto non dimezza la produttività, e tenere le persone occupate al 100% su molti progetti è proprio lo spreco da evitare."],
["b11","What does working at a sustainable pace look like in Scrum?",["Similar intensity in every sprint, with a small rise near each sprint's end","Low intensity early in the release, and very high intensity in its last few sprints","Constant overtime, balanced by a long break after each release","Heavy sprints alternated with an equal number of light sprints"],"Nel sequenziale integrazione e test si concentrano alla fine e l'intensità esplode (notti e weekend). Con Scrum il lavoro è livellato grazie a refactoring, integrazione continua e test automatici."],
["b11","How does Rubin distinguish a team from a group?",["A team collaborates toward a shared vision; a group just shares a common label","A team has a manager who assigns its work; a group organizes itself without any manager","A team is colocated; a group is distributed across different sites","A team works on one product; a group works on several products"],"Scrum richiede team, non gruppi. Un team stabile è un asset: si fidano l'uno dell'altro e hanno uno storico di velocity e stime."],
["b11","Why should organizations move well-formed teams to new products instead of moving people?",["Long-lived teams keep their trust, working agreements and velocity history","Moving people between teams is forbidden by the rules of Scrum","Teams that change product always increase their velocity","New teams go through the forming and storming phases faster than long-lived ones"],"Ricerca di Katz e Staats: team di lunga durata più produttivi. Un gruppo nuovo deve passare per forming, storming, norming, performing. Eccezioni: team disfunzionali o split-and-seed."],
// ---------- Chapter 12: Scrum Team Structures ----------
["b12","What is a feature team?",["A cross-functional, cross-component team that can complete end-customer features from the backlog","A team that owns one component and builds the pieces of features that the other teams request from it","A team of specialists sharing one skill, such as a community of practice of testers","A temporary team formed around one feature and disbanded as soon as it is released"],"La feature team prende una feature dal product backlog e la completa. La component team (o asset, subsystem team) lavora su un solo componente e consegna solo pezzi."],
["b12","Which mix of teams does Rubin see in most large, successful Scrum organizations?",["Mostly feature teams, with an occasional component team when its economics make sense","Mostly component teams, with an occasional feature team for urgent customer requests","Only component teams, so that every component has exactly one trusted owner","Only feature teams, since component teams are not allowed by the Scrum framework"],"Scrum preferisce le feature team. Le organizzazioni che fanno il contrario (quasi tutte component team) pagano in ritardi e flusso interrotto."],
["b12","Why do component teams increase delivery risk for a customer feature?",["The feature needs pieces from several teams with their own priorities, so it is late if any one is late","Component teams cannot estimate their work, so their velocity is always unpredictable","Component teams have no product owner, so the order of their backlog is never defined","Component teams must always use longer sprints than feature teams, by definition of the Scrum framework"],"Ogni pezzo della feature finisce nel backlog di un team diverso, con priorità diverse: basta che uno sia in ritardo e la feature è in ritardo («tutti hanno finito tranne uno»). Le component team hanno un loro PO, sanno stimare e non sono obbligate a sprint più lunghi."],
["b12","In the blended model, what is a pollinator and harvester?",["A component-team member on a feature team who spreads knowledge and collects needed changes","A feature-team member who reviews all of the component code before each release goes out","A product owner who collects the change requests coming from several feature teams at once","A ScrumMaster who runs the scrum of scrums across all the feature areas"],"Pollinator: porta la conoscenza del componente nella feature team. Harvester: raccoglie le modifiche necessarie e le coordina con i colleghi della component team. Il fine è la code ownership condivisa."],
["b12","How does Scrum scale to larger development efforts?",["With multiple right-sized Scrum teams","With development teams of up to 30 people","With one team working longer sprints","With one team per phase of the work"],"Scrum scala con più team di dimensione giusta (5-9 persone), coordinati per esempio con scrum of scrums o release train. Team da 30 persone, sprint più lunghi o un team per fase (analisi, codice, test) riportano ai problemi di comunicazione e di passaggi di consegne."],
["b12","How often is a scrum of scrums typically held?",["A few times a week, as needed","Every day, right after each daily scrum","Once per sprint, just before the review","Once per release, at release planning"],"Non ogni giorno ma alcune volte a settimana. Alcuni lo limitano a 15 minuti e rimandano il problem solving; altri continuano dopo i 15 minuti per risolvere i problemi."],
["b12","Who represents a team at the scrum of scrums?",["The member who can best speak to the inter-team dependencies at that time","The team's product owner, who decides the priorities between teams","Every member of the team, so that everyone stays fully informed","Only the ScrumMaster, who reports the progress of the team to management each time"],"Ogni team sceglie chi mandare in base alle dipendenze del momento; a volte va anche lo ScrumMaster, senza far crescere troppo il numero dei partecipanti."],
["b12","Which questions are answered at a scrum of scrums?",["What my team did and will do that could affect other teams, and where it needs their help","What each person did yesterday, will do today, and what is personally blocking them","Which items each team finished, and what velocity each team achieved in the sprint","Which defects each team found, and which team is responsible for fixing each one"],"Al scrum of scrums si rispondono le tre domande del daily scrum, ma a livello di team e centrate sulle dipendenze: cosa ha fatto e farà il mio team che può influire sugli altri, e dove ci serve il loro aiuto. Lo stato del singolo, la velocity o chi deve correggere i difetti non sono il punto."],
["b12","What is a release train?",["Aligning many teams on a common cadence, with fixed release dates that wait for no one","Releasing each team's increment as soon as it is ready, without any common schedule across teams","A series of hardening sprints at the end of each release, one per feature area","A chain of teams where each team passes its output to the next, like a pipeline"],"La metafora: il treno parte all'orario pubblicato e non aspetta nessuno; chi lo perde prende il prossimo, che parte a un orario noto."],
["b12","In Leffingwell's release train, how often are potentially shippable increments (PSIs) typically available?",["Every 60 to 120 days","Every two-week sprint","Every 6 to 12 months","Every day, via continuous deployment"],"Regole di Leffingwell: date di planning e di PSI fisse, iterazioni di lunghezza comune, integrazione continua a livello di sistema, un PSI ogni 60-120 giorni, eventuali iterazioni di hardening. Ogni sprint è troppo frequente per un treno di molti team, 6-12 mesi troppo raro."],
["b12","In a release train, how are the sprints of the participating teams organized?",["All teams use identical sprint lengths, aligned to start and end on the same dates","Each team chooses its own sprint length, and the train waits for the slowest team","Teams use staggered sprints, so that a different team finishes every week","Sprint lengths double as the release date approaches, to reduce overhead"],"Nel release train tutti i team hanno sprint della stessa durata, allineati sulle stesse date: così ci si sincronizza anche tra feature area. Dopo un numero fisso di sprint (quattro in figura 12.5) c'è un PSI. Il treno non aspetta nessuno, quindi durate diverse o sfasate non funzionano."],
// ---------- Chapter 13: Managers ----------
["b13","According to the 2011 agile survey cited in Chapter 13, what is the number-one impediment to adopting Scrum?",["The feeling of a loss of management control","Development teams opposed to the change","Regulatory compliance in the industry","The inability to scale to large products"],"Figura 13.1 (VersionOne 2011): il primo ostacolo è la sensazione di perdere il controllo da parte del management, al 33%, alla pari con la mancanza di pianificazione up-front. Opposizione del team, normative e scalabilità sono più in basso. Rubin risponde che i manager restano importanti, con un ruolo diverso."],
["b13","Which four groups of responsibilities does Rubin give to functional managers in a Scrum organization?",["Fashioning teams, nurturing teams, aligning and adapting the environment, managing value-creation flow","Assigning tasks, tracking hours, approving designs, running the daily scrum of every team","Writing requirements, ordering the backlog, accepting the work, signing off each release","Hiring developers, setting sprint lengths, estimating backlog items, reviewing each line of code"],"Figura 13.2: dare forma ai team, farli crescere, allineare e adattare l'ambiente, gestire il flusso di creazione del valore. Assegnare task, ordinare il backlog, stimare o fissare la durata degli sprint spettano invece a team, PO o Scrum team."],
["b13","In Rubin's sandbox analogy, what do managers decide and what do teams decide?",["Managers define the sandboxes and who plays in each; teams self-organize inside them","Teams choose their sandboxes and members; managers decide how each castle is built","Managers design every sand castle; teams only build what the design specifies","The product owner chooses the sandboxes; managers decide the daily tasks in each"],"I manager definiscono prodotti e progetti (le sabbiere) e decidono chi gioca in ognuna; dentro i confini il team si auto-organizza. Non decidono come costruire il castello né i task quotidiani, e i team non scelgono da soli sabbiere e compagni."],
["b13","Who usually forms Scrum teams, according to Chapter 13?",["Managers, with input from team members, balancing business needs and constraints","The team members themselves, who select one another without any management input","The ScrumMaster, who interviews candidates and chooses the best combination","The product owner, who picks the people whose skills fit the product backlog"],"I team non si formano da soli: i manager funzionali scelgono insieme le persone dalle varie aree per creare team cross-funzionali con buone competenze a T. I membri possono dare input."],
["b13","Fred performs poorly and hurts the team. Teammates and then the ScrumMaster try to help, without success. What happens next?",["It escalates to Fred's resource manager, who has the authority to move or let Fred go","The ScrumMaster removes Fred from the team, since the ScrumMaster has hiring authority","The team votes Fred out of the team at the next sprint retrospective","The product owner decides whether Fred stays, since Fred affects the backlog"],"Lo ScrumMaster non può assumere né licenziare. Il resource manager, consultando ScrumMaster e team, può spostare Fred o avviare un piano di miglioramento."],
["b13","In Appelo's seven levels of authority, what does level 6, \"inquire\", mean?",["The manager asks about the decision after the team has made it","The manager makes the decision and simply tells the team","The manager and the team make the decision together","The manager collects input before making the decision alone"],"Inquire (livello 6): il team decide e il manager chiede poi informazioni sulla decisione; esempio del libro: la durata dello sprint. Tell è decidere e comunicare, agree decidere insieme, consult raccogliere input e poi decidere da soli. Il livello 7, delegate, lascia tutto al team (per esempio le linee guida di codifica)."],
["b13","How often should managers give feedback to their direct reports in a Scrum organization?",["Much more often than yearly, for example every sprint, in the context of team performance","Once a year, during the annual performance review, exactly as in traditional organizations","Never: in Scrum, feedback to individuals is given only by the ScrumMaster","Only when a person is clearly underperforming and the team asks for it"],"Il feedback una o due volte l'anno non si allinea ai cicli di apprendimento degli sprint. Meglio allinearne la frequenza agli sprint e collegarlo alla performance del team."],
["b13","What does \"provide functional-area leadership\" mean for a manager such as an art director?",["Setting standards and reviewing work for consistency across teams, without assigning tasks","Assigning the daily tasks of every artist on each Scrum team to keep the style consistent","Acting as the product owner for all art-related items in every team's backlog","Replacing the ScrumMaster of the teams where artists work, to protect the art style"],"L'art director imposta gli standard e rivede il lavoro per evitare un gioco con arte gotica in un team e cartoon in un altro. Non assegna task né dice come lavorare."],
["b13","Why does Rubin suggest alternatives to fixed-price contracts with outsourcers?",["They set the parties at odds: the contractor delivers as little as possible, the client wants as much as possible","They are not allowed for organizations that use Scrum, because the budget of a release must stay flexible","They prevent the outsourced developers from attending the sprint reviews of the client's product","They require a separate project manager on each side, a role that Scrum teams are not allowed to have"],"Allineare i partner fa parte di allineare l'ambiente. Un'alternativa semplice è «affittare» lo Scrum team di un fornitore e lavorare con Scrum come descritto nel libro."],
["b13","Which measure does Rubin suggest to apply \"focus on idle work, not idle workers\" in reporting?",["Cycle time, the time between when work starts and when it finishes","Utilization, the share of hours each person spends on assigned tasks","Lines of code, the amount of code each developer writes per sprint","Hours logged, compared with the hours estimated for each task"],"I manager devono allineare misure e report ai principi agili: il cycle time mostra se il flusso si blocca. Innovation accounting (Ries) misura quanto in fretta si chiude il ciclo di apprendimento."],
["b13","Where do the traditional project manager's responsibilities go in a Scrum organization (Table 13.3)?",["They are distributed among the product owner, ScrumMaster, development team and other managers","They all move to the ScrumMaster, who becomes the project manager of the Scrum team","They all move to the product owner, who becomes accountable for every single project management activity","They disappear, because Scrum makes scope, time, cost and risk management unnecessary"],"Per esempio lo scope: livello macro al PO, livello sprint al team. Un ex project manager può diventare ScrumMaster, product owner (che assume almeno tante responsabilità quanto lo SM) o membro del team."],
["b13","When might an organization keep a separate project manager alongside Scrum teams?",["On very large or multiparty efforts, as a servant coordinator while teams still own coordination","On every project, because Scrum teams are not able to coordinate their dependencies by themselves at all","On small efforts, where a scrum of scrums would be too heavy for a few teams","Never: Rubin says the role must be eliminated as soon as Scrum is adopted"],"Con decine o centinaia di team, o con subappaltatori e gruppi non Scrum, può servire chi si occupa della logistica. Ma deve essere un assistente (servant leader): il coordinamento resta dei team."],
// ---------- Chapter 14: Scrum Planning Principles ----------
["b14","Which statement best captures Scrum's attitude toward planning?",["The act of planning matters more than any single plan, and plans are revisited as the team learns","Planning is minimized, because an adaptive process makes every plan unreliable from the start","A detailed up-front plan is the best protection against uncertainty, with deviations through change control","Only the sprint-level plan is worth creating, since every longer-range plan is just a guess"],"Scrum pianifica in continuo e a più livelli: conta l'attività di pianificare, mentre ogni singolo piano è un'ipotesi da aggiornare. Non riduce la pianificazione al minimo, non si affida a un piano dettagliato con change control e non scarta i piani di lungo periodo, che restano grezzi."],
["b14","How does the level of detail in a plan relate to its time horizon in Scrum?",["Near-term plans are detailed, while longer-term plans stay coarse and are refined as they approach","Plans of every horizon are made at the same level of detail, so that they can be compared","Long-term plans are detailed to reduce risk, while near-term plans are left more flexible to change","The level of detail depends only on the size of the team, not on the time horizon"],"Raffinamento progressivo: i piani vicini sono dettagliati, quelli lontani restano grezzi e si dettagliano quando si avvicinano e si sa di più. Dettagliare tutto allo stesso modo o, peggio, il lungo periodo più del breve significa investire su ipotesi che cambieranno."],
["b14","A team has a fixed delivery date and a fixed team size, and it is behind the original forecast. Which variable should flex first?",["Scope, by dropping or deferring the lowest-value items","Team capacity, by asking the people to work overtime for a while","Sprint length, by extending the remaining sprints","Quality, by shortening or skipping some testing"],"Con data e risorse fisse, varia lo scope: siccome il backlog è ordinato per valore, slittano gli elementi meno importanti. Qualità e ritmo sostenibile non si negoziano."],
["b14","Rubin's friend John, an extreme skier, does not plan his entire run from the top. Which principle does this illustrate?",["Up-front planning should be helpful without being excessive","Keep planning options open forever, never deciding in advance","Plans made at the top are always right, so follow them exactly","Skiing and software both need a complete plan before starting"],"John sceglie un punto lungo la discesa e pianifica due o tre curve: il terreno visto dall'alto inganna e gli imprevisti arrivano. Un po' di pianificazione up-front, non troppa."],
["b14","\"When the map and the terrain don't agree, believe the terrain.\" What does this mean for plans?",["Focus more on adapting and replanning than on conforming to the original plan","Measure progress as the variance between the plan and the actual results","Freeze the plan and treat every deviation as an error to be corrected","Replace plans with improvisation, since maps are always wrong"],"Un piano up-front codifica la nostra ignoranza iniziale. Se la realtà diverge, si aggiorna il piano invece di misurare il successo come aderenza al Gantt."],
["b14","Why does Rubin warn against a detailed up-front Gantt chart, from the point of view of planning inventory?",["Detailed plans made far ahead become waste when things change, like task names 18 months out","Gantt charts are forbidden by the Scrum framework, which only allows burndown and burnup charts","A Gantt chart has too little detail to coordinate the work of several teams","Planning artifacts have no cost, so the problem is only that they look complicated"],"Gli artefatti di pianificazione sono inventario: produrne troppi troppo presto (George assegnato a un task tra 18 mesi) genera spreco quando le ipotesi vengono smentite. Scrum non vieta i Gantt, il problema non è la mancanza di dettaglio, e i piani hanno un costo."],
["b14","In Table 14.2, what happens to the two-year return when the same product is released quarterly instead of once after 12 months?",["The return and the internal rate of return rise, and less cash investment is needed","The return falls, because each additional release costs $100K","The return is unchanged, because the same features are eventually built and sold anyway","The return rises, but the cash investment needed doubles"],"Rilascio unico: IRR 9,1% e investimento di cassa 1,3M. Semestrale: 15,7%. Trimestrale: 19,5% con soli 0,45M investiti. Si raggiungono prima autofinanziamento e pareggio."],
["b14","What limits how small and frequent releases can be?",["Every product has a minimum releasable set of features, and some markets can't absorb frequent releases","Scrum requires each release to last at least one quarter, so as to amortize the fixed cost of each release","Teams cannot release more often than once every three sprints because of the review","Each release must contain all the features of the original product vision"],"Sotto un certo insieme minimo di feature il rilascio non è vendibile; e in alcuni mercati rilasci frequenti non sono possibili. Dove il mercato accetta valore parziale, conviene rilasciare spesso."],
["b14","What does Rubin mean by \"pivoting\", following Ries?",["Changing direction while staying grounded in what has been learned","Returning to the original plan after an unsuccessful experiment","Abandoning the product as soon as any assumption proves false","Switching from Scrum to a plan-driven process when deadlines approach"],"Plan to learn fast and pivot when necessary: nessuna previsione sostituisce il fare, imparare in fretta e correggere rotta se serve."],
// ---------- Chapter 15: Multilevel Planning ----------
["b15","Which sequence orders the planning levels from the broadest to the narrowest horizon?",["Portfolio, product, release, sprint, daily","Portfolio, product, sprint, release, daily","Portfolio, release, product, sprint, daily","Product, portfolio, release, sprint, daily"],"La cipolla della pianificazione va dal portfolio, al prodotto (visione e roadmap), alla release, fino a sprint e giorno. Sopra a tutto c'è la strategia, fuori dallo scope del libro."],
["b15","Which statement about the relationship between planning levels is correct?",["Outer levels give context, and what inner levels learn (velocity, feedback) adjusts outer plans","Each outer-level plan must be completed and formally approved before work at the inner level can start","Inner levels only execute outer plans and never provide any feedback to them","Each level is owned by a different group, with no coordination between the levels"],"La pianificazione è iterativa: i livelli esterni danno il contesto e ciò che si impara in quelli interni (velocity, feedback della review) torna su ad aggiornarli. Non si aspetta che un piano esterno sia finito e approvato, e i livelli non sono compartimenti senza coordinamento."],
["b15","Which statement best describes a release plan in Scrum?",["A forecast of what will likely be delivered by when, based on velocity and updated as the team learns","A detailed task list covering every sprint in the release, prepared during the first sprint planning","A risk register that the ScrumMaster maintains and reviews with the stakeholders each sprint","A baselined commitment of scope, date and cost that cannot change once it has been approved"],"Il piano di release è una previsione, aggiornata ogni sprint man mano che velocity e priorità diventano più chiare."],
["b15","According to Table 15.1, what is the typical horizon of release planning?",["Three (or fewer) to nine months","One week to one calendar month","Possibly a year or more","Every day of the sprint"],"Tabella 15.1: release da tre (o meno) a nove mesi. Da una settimana a un mese è lo sprint, un anno o più il portfolio, ogni giorno il livello giornaliero."],
["b15","According to Table 15.1, who is involved in release planning?",["The entire Scrum team and the stakeholders","The product owner and the stakeholders only","The ScrumMaster and the development team only","The stakeholders and the portfolio managers"],"Release planning: tutto lo Scrum team con gli stakeholder. Portfolio: stakeholder e product owner. Daily: ScrumMaster e development team."],
["b15","Which planning levels does Scrum formally define?",["Only sprint planning and daily planning","All five levels, from portfolio to daily","Portfolio, release and sprint planning","Only release and sprint planning"],"Scrum definisce formalmente solo sprint planning e daily scrum; Rubin consiglia anche portfolio, product e release planning perché utili alla maggior parte delle organizzazioni."],
["b15","What are the deliverables of product planning (envisioning)?",["Product vision, roadmap and high-level features","Sprint goal and sprint backlog","Portfolio backlog and list of in-process products","Release plan with a burndown chart"],"Envisioning: visione, product backlog di alto livello, spesso una roadmap. Portfolio: portfolio backlog e prodotti in corso. Sprint: sprint goal e sprint backlog."],
["b15","What is a product roadmap?",["An overview of how the product will be built and delivered incrementally across releases","A detailed Gantt chart of all the tasks needed to complete the whole product vision, release by release","The list of sprint goals for the next twelve sprints of every team","The technical architecture diagram that guides the development team"],"La roadmap (detta anche release roadmap) mostra la natura incrementale dei rilasci nel tempo. Rubin usa il formato di Luke Hohmann. Anche con deployment continuo può aiutare a ragionare per gruppi di feature."],
["b15","In the Scrum Alliance website example, what kinds of releases did the initial roadmap contain?",["A fixed-scope release (0.5) and a fixed-date release (1.0)","Two fixed-date releases with the same scope","Two fixed-scope releases with no dates","One single release with fixed scope, fixed date and fixed budget"],"Nel 2006 il sito della Scrum Alliance era pessimo; Rubin, product owner, pianificò una release 0.5 a scope fisso e una 1.0 a data fissa."],
// ---------- Chapter 16: Portfolio Planning ----------
["b16","When does portfolio planning take place?",["It is never-ending: as long as there are products, it continues","Once a year, during the annual strategic-planning event","Once per product, before its first release is approved","Only when a new product idea clears the strategic filter"],"Il portfolio planning è continuo: gestisce prodotti nuovi e prodotti in corso, a intervalli regolari e quando succedono eventi rilevanti."],
["b16","Who participates in portfolio planning?",["Internal stakeholders, the product owners and, often, senior architects","The full Scrum teams of all the products, together with their ScrumMasters","Only the product owners, who vote on each other's products","Only the CEO and the chief financial officer of the company"],"Stakeholder interni con una visione ampia del business (a volte un comitato di approvazione), i product owner dei prodotti e, spesso, architetti e tech lead per i vincoli tecnici."],
["b16","What are the two outputs of portfolio planning?",["A portfolio backlog of approved future products and a set of active products","A release plan for each product and a sprint backlog for each team","A product vision and a product roadmap for each of the candidate products in the list","A list of rejected ideas and a budget for the next fiscal year"],"Portfolio backlog: prodotti approvati ma non ancora iniziati, in ordine. Prodotti attivi: quelli da iniziare subito e quelli in corso approvati per continuare."],
["b16","Two products have different costs of delay and different durations. Which scheduling approach fits best?",["Weighted shortest job first: cost of delay divided by duration","Shortest job first, ignoring the cost of delay of each product","Highest cost of delay first, ignoring the duration of each product","First come, first served, in the order the ideas were approved"],"Stesso costo del ritardo e durate diverse: shortest job first. Stessa durata e costi diversi: prima il costo del ritardo più alto. Entrambi variabili (il caso normale): WSJF."],
["b16","Project A has a cost of delay of $5K per month and project B of $75K per month. What does this mean for sequencing?",["Doing B first can greatly improve the lifecycle profits of the whole portfolio","A should go first, since its lower cost of delay makes it less risky","The order doesn't matter, since both projects will be finished eventually","B should be cancelled, since its high cost of delay shows it is badly planned"],"Tabella 16.2: ignorare il costo del ritardo porta a sequenze sbagliate. L'obiettivo è massimizzare i profitti sull'intero ciclo di vita del portfolio, non del singolo prodotto."],
["b16","Which cost-of-delay profile describes a product with zero delay cost until a certain date, then a large one?",["Fixed date","Linear","Logarithmic","Intangible"],"Fixed date: nessun costo del ritardo fino a una data (per esempio un obbligo normativo o un evento), poi un costo alto. Linear cresce a ritmo costante, logarithmic accumula gran parte del costo subito, intangible non ha un costo apparente oggi (per esempio il debito tecnico)."],
["b16","Why does Rubin estimate portfolio items with T-shirt sizes such as M = $50K to $125K?",["They are accurate enough for portfolio decisions without false precision","Exact dollar amounts are forbidden in portfolio planning","T-shirt sizes can be converted to story points automatically","Stakeholders cannot understand estimates expressed in dollars at this early stage"],"Accuracy, not precision: le taglie (XS 10-25K, S 25-50K, M 50-125K, L 125-350K, XL oltre 350K) sono abbastanza accurate per decidere, e una stima «media» era utile quanto un falso preciso $72.381,27. Non è vietato usare dollari e le taglie non si convertono automaticamente in punti."],
["b16","What should a good economic filter do?",["Quickly approve opportunities with overwhelming value relative to cost, and reject most others","Approve every product that has a positive expected return, however small it is","Delay all decisions until a detailed business case is available for each and every idea submitted","Approve products in the order the stakeholders submitted them, to be fair"],"Se il valore sovrasta nettamente i costi si approva senza discutere; se si litiga su piccole differenze, il supporto economico non è schiacciante e si rifiuta."],
["b16","How can an organization balance the arrival rate of products with the departure rate?",["Evaluate opportunities more often, for example monthly, instead of in one annual event","Approve all ideas once a year, so that the portfolio backlog is full for the whole year","Start every approved product immediately, so that nothing waits in the backlog","Freeze the portfolio backlog until all in-process products are completed"],"Un evento annuale riversa tutto insieme nel portfolio backlog e lo sovraccarica. Valutare le opportunità più spesso (ogni mese, o almeno ogni trimestre) bilancia arrivi e partenze e permette di cogliere quelle emergenti. Avviare tutto subito o congelare il backlog crea il problema opposto."],
["b16","What is the convoy effect in portfolio planning?",["A large product hogs shared resources, delaying many smaller products queued behind it","Several teams move together from product to product, like a convoy of trucks","Products are released together in one big release to save on release costs","Small products are always finished first, starving the large ones of resources"],"Come un trattore su una strada di campagna a una corsia: un prodotto grande occupa le risorse condivise e tiene in coda tanti prodotti piccoli, che accumulano costo del ritardo. Rimedio: rilasci più piccoli e frequenti (un'organizzazione rifiutava ogni progetto più lungo di nove mesi). Non riguarda team che si spostano insieme."],
["b16","A smart restaurateur won't seat guests when several waiters are sick. Which outflow strategy does this illustrate?",["Establish a WIP limit, never starting more products than there is capacity to complete","Focus on idle workers, so that every waiter stays fully busy all evening","Quickly embrace emergent opportunities, seating every guest who arrives","Use marginal economics, deciding which tables to close down in the middle of the dinner service"],"Il ristoratore non fa sedere clienti se non ha camerieri per servirli: è il WIP limit, non iniziare più prodotti di quanti si possano completare. Tenere tutti i camerieri occupati o far sedere tutti peggiora il servizio per tutti; l'unità di capacità per il WIP limit è il team."],
["b16","Only two developers are free. Should the organization start a new product with them?",["No: wait for a complete Scrum team, since an incomplete team can't get features done","Yes: starting early is always better than leaving developers idle, even for a few weeks","Yes, as long as the product owner is also available to them","No: wait until all the teams for the whole product are free"],"Bisogna aspettare un team Scrum completo: due sviluppatori da soli non riescono a portare feature a done e bloccano lavoro su altri prodotti. Partire per non lasciare persone ferme è l'errore «idle workers»; non serve però aspettare tutti i team: si può partire con un team completo e aggiungere gli altri."],
["b16","When deciding whether to continue an in-process product, how should the money already spent be treated?",["As a sunk cost: only the marginal economics of the next step matter","As an investment that must be recovered by finishing the product","As the main criterion: the more spent, the more reason to continue","As a reason to terminate, since past spending shows poor planning"],"Marginal economics: ci si chiede solo se la prossima spesa è giustificata dal ritorno. Le opzioni sono preserve, pivot, deliver, terminate, valutate per esempio a fine sprint."],
// ---------- Chapter 17: Envisioning (Product Planning) ----------
["b17","What does an idea go through before and after envisioning, according to Figure 17.1?",["Strategic filter, then envisioning, then the portfolio's economic filter","Economic filter, then envisioning, then the organization's strategic filter","Release planning, then envisioning, then sprint planning","Envisioning, then strategic filter, then release planning"],"L'idea deve prima essere coerente con la strategia (strategic filter); poi si fa l'envisioning; il risultato entra nel portfolio planning, dove si applica il filtro economico. L'envisioning è continuo."],
["b17","Who is the only required participant in initial envisioning?",["The product owner","The ScrumMaster","The development team","The chief executive"],"All'envisioning iniziale è obbligatorio solo il product owner; di solito partecipano anche stakeholder interni e specialisti (ricerca di mercato, business case, UX, architettura). ScrumMaster e development team non servono finché il prodotto non è approvato, e il CEO non è un partecipante richiesto."],
["b17","What is the confidence threshold in envisioning?",["The information decision makers need to make a go/no-go funding decision","The minimum velocity a team must reach before development starts","The percentage of features that must be estimated before the first sprint","The approval level the ScrumMaster gives to the product vision"],"La soglia di confidenza è l'informazione che serve ai decisori per decidere se finanziare o no lo sviluppo: la «definition of done» dell'envisioning. Velocity minima, percentuale di feature stimate o un'approvazione dello ScrumMaster non c'entrano."],
["b17","Which of these is a vision format listed in Table 17.1?",["A fictitious magazine review of the product","A detailed requirements specification","A complete Gantt chart of the release","A signed contract with the first customer"],"Formati: elevator statement, product datasheet, product vision box, slide per la user conference, comunicato stampa, recensione fittizia su una rivista. La visione non è un documento di centinaia di pagine."],
["b17","Which vision format did the SR4U team choose?",["A press release","An elevator statement","A product vision box","A product datasheet"],"Roger e gli stakeholder scrivono il comunicato stampa che vorrebbero pubblicare al lancio, partendo dalle aree di valore (risparmio di tempo, differenziazione, spiazzare i concorrenti). Elevator statement, vision box e datasheet sono altri formati della tabella 17.1, non scelti per SR4U."],
["b17","What did the SR4U knowledge-acquisition sprint do during envisioning?",["Subject matter experts manually filtered reviews to test whether users preferred filtered results","The full Scrum team built a first automated filtering engine to measure both its speed and its accuracy","Market researchers surveyed users about the price they would pay for the service","Architects compared two cloud providers for hosting the review-filtering service"],"Prima di investire in un sistema automatico, gli esperti producevano a mano i risultati filtrati e i clienti li confrontavano con quelli non filtrati: validated learning a basso costo."],
["b17","What are minimum releasable features (MRFs)?",["The smallest set of must-have features needed to meet customer value and quality expectations","The features that the team can surely finish within the first sprint of the release, with no risk","The features that remain after removing everything the stakeholders disagree about","The lowest-priority features, which are released only if there is time left over"],"Chiamate anche MVP o MMF. Si può consegnare di più, ma consegnare meno non darebbe abbastanza valore. Alcune organizzazioni le combinano con rilasci fissi e periodici (per esempio ogni trimestre)."],
["b17","How far into the future did Roger's SR4U product roadmap extend, and why?",["Nine months: further would rest on too many unvalidated assumptions","Several years: the whole vision needed to be mapped before funding","One sprint: anything further is waste in a Scrum organization","Three years: the approval committee requires long-range plans"],"La visione richiede anni, ma con poco validated learning e un mercato veloce Roger sceglie una roadmap di nove mesi, centrata sulla prima release (in tempo per la Social Media Expo). Mappare anni sarebbe basare ipotesi su ipotesi; uno sprint solo sarebbe troppo poco per una roadmap."],
["b17","What happens when the confidence threshold bar is set too high?",["Effort and delay grow while uncertainty remains, giving only an illusion of certainty","Decisions become fully certain, so all the later sprint reviews can be skipped","The product is funded faster, because the stakeholders trust very detailed analyses more","The team can start development earlier, since the requirements are all complete"],"Oltre un certo punto, più analisi non aggiunge certezza perché il futuro resta incerto: si spende di più e si ritarda, decidendo sotto un'illusione di certezza."],
["b17","What does \"use incremental/provisional funding\" mean?",["Fund enough development to acquire the next critical knowledge, then decide again","Fund the whole product up front, then release the money in equal tranches","Fund only the features that customers have already paid for in advance","Fund each sprint only if its velocity is higher than the previous one"],"Non si cerca di approvare tutto lo sviluppo futuro: si finanzia il prossimo passo (per esempio la release 1) e si ridecide con informazioni migliori."],
["b17","Why should envisioning be done quickly?",["The market clock starts with the idea: long envisioning delays delivery and costs money","Scrum forbids envisioning activities that last longer than one sprint","Stakeholders lose interest if the vision document takes too long to write and to review","Quick envisioning lets the team skip release planning entirely"],"Act quickly: il tempo di envisioning conta nel tempo di consegna. Prima si costruisce qualcosa di tangibile, prima si validano le ipotesi."],
// ---------- Chapter 18: Release Planning ----------
["b18","How often does release planning take place in Scrum?",["Initially for a day or two, then every sprint as the plan is updated","Once, at the start of the release, and then never revisited","Only at the end of each release, to plan the content of the following one","Every day, during the daily scrum of each development team"],"La release iniziale si pianifica in uno o due giorni; poi il piano si aggiorna in ogni sprint, per esempio alla sprint review, man mano che arriva validated learning. Non è un piano fatto una volta sola né a fine release, e il daily scrum è un'altra cosa."],
["b18","Who takes part in release planning?",["The stakeholders and the full Scrum team","The product owner and the stakeholders only","The ScrumMaster and the managers only","The development team and the architects only"],"Al release planning partecipano gli stakeholder e tutto lo Scrum team, perché servono compromessi di business e tecnici per bilanciare valore e qualità. Senza il team mancherebbero le stime e i vincoli tecnici, senza gli stakeholder le priorità di business."],
["b18","Which release constraint combinations does Rubin label \"not recommended\" in Table 18.1?",["Fixed everything, and fixed scope and date","Fixed scope, and fixed date","Fixed date only, with a flexible scope and budget","Fixed scope only, with a flexible date"],"Fixed everything e fixed scope and date sono sconsigliati. Fixed scope (data flessibile) e fixed date (scope flessibile) sono praticabili; Rubin preferisce la data fissa."],
["b18","Fixed scope and date with a flexible budget often leads to adding people late. What does Rubin quote?",["Brooks: adding manpower to a late software project makes it later","Parkinson: work expands to fill the time available for its completion","Conway: systems mirror the communication structure of organizations","Hofstadter: it always takes longer than you expect, even accounting for it"],"Rubin cita Brooks: aggiungere persone a un progetto software in ritardo lo ritarda ancora di più, perché i nuovi vanno formati e aumentano i canali di comunicazione («nove donne non fanno un bambino in un mese»). Parkinson, Conway e Hofstadter sono leggi vere ma non quella citata qui."],
["b18","Why does Rubin call the fixed-scope model \"fixed scope\" and not \"fixed scope and budget\"?",["If more time is needed for the fixed scope, the budget grows too","Because the budget is always fixed by the portfolio planning process","Because the scope can grow freely while the budget stays the same","Because budget is not a constraint that Scrum teams ever consider"],"Se lo scope è fisso e servono più sprint per completarlo, crescono anche i costi del personale: per questo Rubin parla di scope fisso e non di scope e budget fissi. Il budget non è fissato dal portfolio, lo scope non cresce liberamente e il budget resta un vincolo importante."],
["b18","Which release constraint model does Rubin consider most aligned with Scrum principles?",["Fixed date, with fixed budget and flexible scope","Fixed scope, with flexible date and budget","Fixed scope and date, with flexible budget","Fixed everything, with flexible quality"],"Con data e budget fissi, lo scope si adatta: siccome si costruisce prima ciò che vale di più, quello che manca alla fine ha poco valore. Funziona se le feature importanti sono davvero done."],
["b18","What happens if scope, date and budget are all over-constrained?",["Quality becomes the flexible variable, leading to dissatisfied customers or technical debt","The team simply works faster, because a tighter plan motivates people","The ScrumMaster negotiates a longer sprint length to absorb the extra work","The product owner may drop the definition of done for all of the remaining sprints of the release"],"Variable quality: se si vincolano scope, data e budget, a cedere è la qualità, con debito tecnico e un prodotto che delude i clienti. Il team non va magicamente più veloce, e allungare gli sprint o abbandonare la definition of done sono proprio i modi in cui la qualità viene sacrificata."],
["b18","Who is ultimately responsible for defining the minimum releasable features (MRFs) of a release?",["The product owner","The development team","The ScrumMaster","The release manager"],"Le minimum releasable features le definisce il product owner, con gli stakeholder, e le rivede di continuo; se sono poco chiare è difficile decidere cosa tagliare quando il tempo stringe. Team e ScrumMaster contribuiscono con stime e facilitazione, non decidono il contenuto minimo."],
["b18","SR4U Release 1.0 has six two-week sprints, and the team's velocity is 18 to 22 points. How much work can it plan for?",["Between 108 and 132 story points","Between 90 and 110 story points","Exactly 120 story points","Between 18 and 22 story points"],"Release a data fissa: 6 sprint × 18 = 108 punti (linea will-have), 6 × 22 = 132 (linea might-have). 90-110 sbaglia i conti, 120 è la media (un numero solo), 18-22 è la velocity di un singolo sprint."],
["b18","In fixed-date release planning, what are the \"will-have\" and \"might-have\" lines?",["The points reached using the slower and the faster velocity times the number of sprints","The must-have items and the nice-to-have items, as chosen by the stakeholders at the start","The items in the current sprint and the items in the next sprint","The items estimated by the team and the items not yet estimated"],"Tabella 18.2: velocity lenta per numero di sprint, si conta nel backlog e si traccia la linea will-have; velocity veloce, linea might-have. Sotto la seconda, won't-have."],
["b18","A fixed-scope release has 150 story points and the velocity is 18 to 22 points per two-week sprint. What is the forecast?",["Seven to nine sprints, that is 14 to 18 weeks","Eight sprints exactly, that is 16 weeks","Six to eight sprints, that is 12 to 16 weeks","Nine to eleven sprints, that is 18 to 22 weeks"],"Tabella 18.3: 150 / 22 = 6,8, arrotondato a 7; 150 / 18 = 8,3, arrotondato a 9; con sprint di due settimane, da 14 a 18 settimane. Un numero esatto nasconde l'incertezza, 6-8 arrotonda per difetto. Il costo si ottiene moltiplicando il numero di sprint per il costo di uno sprint."],
["b18","Why are traditional release burndown and burnup charts not effective for fixed-date releases?",["They assume a known total scope, but a fixed-date release forecasts a range of scope","They need the total number of sprints, which is not known in advance in a fixed-date release","They work only with ideal days, while fixed-date releases use story points","They can show progress only for releases with a single development team"],"Per la data fissa si mostra il range di feature previsto, aggiornato a ogni sprint; oppure un burnup con il backlog capovolto (figura 18.14)."],
["b18","Why do some people prefer a release burnup chart to a burndown chart?",["It shows a change in scope easily, by moving the target line","It is the only release chart that the Scrum framework allows teams to use","It needs no velocity data to show the release status","It shows the hours each person has logged per sprint"],"Nel burnup la linea obiettivo sale quando si aggiunge scope, e si vede subito che il progresso non è cambiato; nel burndown si può fare ma è meno immediato. Entrambi richiedono la velocity per le proiezioni, nessuno dei due è imposto da Scrum e nessuno mostra le ore delle persone."],
["b18","What is sprint mapping (PBI slotting)?",["An early, rough slotting of near-term backlog items into upcoming sprints","The final selection of items for a sprint, which is made during sprint planning","Assigning each product backlog item to the developer who will build it","Mapping each sprint to a calendar month for financial reporting"],"La scelta definitiva avviene allo sprint planning, ma una mappatura rapida di qualche sprint aiuta a coordinare più team e a vedere le dipendenze."],
// ---------- Chapter 19: Sprint Planning ----------
["b19","How does Rubin describe two-part sprint planning?",["Part 1 (what): forecast items that fill the capacity; part 2 (how): gain confidence by planning tasks","Part 1 (how): the ScrumMaster plans the tasks; part 2 (what): the product owner picks the items","Part 1: the stakeholders choose the items; part 2: the product owner assigns the hours to each developer","Part 1: the items of the next three sprints are sized; part 2: the release plan is then updated"],"Parte 1: il team stabilisce la capacità e prevede quali PBI può consegnare (se può fare 40 punti, ne sceglie circa 40). Parte 2: scompone in task stimati in ore e confronta con la capacità."],
["b19","What characterizes one-part sprint planning, the approach Rubin sees most often?",["The team interleaves selecting one item and gaining confidence in it, until capacity runs out","The team selects all items up to its velocity first, then plans their tasks at the very end","The product owner selects the items, and the team only estimates the hours of each task","The ScrumMaster calculates capacity and chooses the items that fit, then the team agrees"],"Nell'approccio in una parte si determina la capacità, si seleziona un elemento, ci si assicura che ci stia e si ripete fino a esaurire la capacità. Selezionare tutto e pianificare dopo è l'approccio in due parti; selezione e capacità non le decidono da soli PO o ScrumMaster."],
["b19","What is the ScrumMaster's role at sprint planning?",["Observe, ask probing questions and facilitate, possibly challenging an unrealistic commitment","Decide how much work the development team should commit to in the coming sprint, based on velocity","Present the prioritized product backlog and the initial sprint goal to the team","Assign each task to the team member who is best suited to complete it"],"Lo ScrumMaster non è a capo del team e non decide l'impegno al suo posto, ma può metterlo in discussione perché sia realistico. Il PO presenta backlog e goal iniziale."],
["b19","Which rule of thumb do teams often use when breaking items into tasks?",["No task should be more than about eight hours of effort","No task should be smaller than one ideal day of effort","Each task should correspond to exactly one story point","Each task should take one developer the whole sprint"],"Con task di al massimo otto ore circa il team capisce bene cosa serve e se ci sta nel tempo disponibile. Task da almeno un giorno o da un intero sprint sono troppo grossi per vedere i problemi, e i task si stimano in ore, non in story point."],
["b19","On a two-week sprint, roughly how much time is reserved for sprint planning, review and retrospective together?",["About one day","About half an hour","About three days","About a full week"],"In uno sprint di dieci giorni circa un giorno va a sprint planning, review e retrospective, e fino al 10% al grooming; poi si tolgono impegni esterni, ferie e un buffer. Mezz'ora è troppo poco per queste attività, tre giorni o una settimana toglierebbero troppo tempo allo sprint."],
["b19","A team's average velocity is 40 points, but the next sprint falls during the December holidays. What capacity should it plan with?",["Something closer to 20 points, reflecting the time off","40 points, since the average velocity is always the target","50 points, to make up for the holidays in advance","Zero: no sprint should be run during holidays"],"Si parte dalla velocity media o da quella dello sprint precedente («yesterday's weather») e la si corregge se lo sprint sarà diverso dal solito."],
["b19","A team's effort-hour capacity is 140 to 197 hours. What does Rubin advise about the commitment?",["Probably more than 140 but certainly less than 197 hours, keeping a buffer","Exactly 197 hours, so that the whole capacity is used","Exactly 140 hours, never going above the lower bound","About 250 hours, since the task estimates of most teams are usually too high"],"Meglio impegnarsi su qualcosa più di 140 ore ma certamente meno di 197: prendere 197 ore non lascerebbe nessun buffer per imprevisti e stime sbagliate. Fermarsi esattamente a 140 non è una regola, e superare la capacità sperando che le stime siano gonfiate è un impegno irrealistico."],
["b19","A commitment of 150 effort-hours fits the capacity, but Simon is the only UI person and has just 14 to 21 hours. What is the risk?",["The team may run out of UI capacity even though its total capacity looks fine","There is no risk, because the total hours are comfortably within capacity","Simon must leave the team, since part-time members are not allowed in Scrum","The ScrumMaster must reassign all UI tasks to other developers immediately"],"In sprint planning non si assegnano i task alle persone, ma bisogna almeno considerare la capacità per competenza, altrimenti si fa un impegno sbagliato."],
["b19","What does \"don't start what you can't finish\" mean when selecting items?",["If the next item won't fit, split it into smaller valuable items or pick another that fits","Start every top item anyway, and carry any unfinished work over to the next sprint","Never select an item larger than the smallest item already in the sprint backlog","Start only the items that the product owner has already accepted as done"],"Limitare il WIP: un lavoro iniziato e non finito genera sprechi, e il riporto da uno sprint all'altro impedisce un incremento potenzialmente rilasciabile."],
["b19","Should tasks be assigned to specific people during sprint planning?",["Generally no: consider skills capacity, then let people pick tasks during the sprint","Yes: every task gets a name, so that the accountability is clear from the very first day","Yes: the ScrumMaster assigns tasks to balance the workload of each person","No: the product owner assigns them at the first daily scrum of the sprint"],"In sprint planning in genere non si assegnano i task alle persone: basta controllare la capacità per competenza, poi ognuno sceglie i task durante lo sprint, just in time. Assegnarli prima è spesso uno spreco (le cose cambiano) e riduce la proprietà collettiva; né ScrumMaster né PO assegnano task."],
["b19","Does Rubin use the word \"commitment\" or \"forecast\" for what the team will deliver?",["Commitment, though the planning approaches are the same with either term","Forecast, because no team can commit to anything uncertain","Commitment, meaning that the sprint is extended whenever the work is not done","Forecast for one-part planning, commitment for two-part planning"],"Rubin preferisce commitment; la Scrum Guide del 2011 parla di forecast. La differenza riguarda solo le sfumature su scope e gestione delle novità durante lo sprint."],
// ---------- Chapter 20: Sprint Execution ----------
["b20","What is the main purpose of the daily scrum?",["For the team to inspect progress toward the sprint goal and plan the next day's work","For the product owner to reprioritize the items in the sprint backlog every day","For the team to resolve every impediment in detail, so no follow-up discussions are needed","For the ScrumMaster to collect each person's status and report it to management"],"Il daily scrum serve al team per ispezionare l'avanzamento verso il goal e pianificare la giornata: è ispezione, sincronizzazione e pianificazione adattiva. Non è il momento in cui il PO riordina il lavoro, non risolve ogni impedimento nel dettaglio e non è un report per il management."],
["b20","A sprint burnup chart in story points stays nearly flat for most of the sprint and rises steeply only near the end. What does this most likely indicate?",["Bad flow: too many items started at once, or large items, so few finish until late","The product owner added several new items to the sprint halfway through","The sprint goal became obsolete, so the team stopped working on the tasks","The team estimated accurately at the start of the sprint and is now well ahead of its plan"],"È la linea «Bad flow» della figura 20.9: troppi PBI in parallelo, completamenti rimandati a fine sprint, elementi grandi. Problemi che emergono tardi."],
["b20","Which approach best supports finishing items before starting new ones?",["Members swarm on items in priority order, finishing what is started before starting more","Design and coding are done for all items first, with testing grouped at the end of the sprint","The ScrumMaster assigns each item to the most specialized person who is available","Each developer starts a separate item, so that everyone is kept fully busy at all times"],"Lo swarming su pochi elementi in ordine di priorità limita il WIP e produce elementi done in continuo. Progettare e codificare tutto prima e testare alla fine è un mini-waterfall, assegnare per specialità crea code, e tenere tutti occupati su elementi diversi moltiplica il lavoro a metà."],
["b20","When does sprint execution start and end?",["It starts after sprint planning and ends when the sprint review starts","It starts after the first daily scrum and ends at the retrospective","It starts with grooming and ends when the release is shipped","It starts after the sprint review and ends at the next planning"],"L'esecuzione dello sprint inizia dopo lo sprint planning e finisce quando inizia la review: è la maggior parte dello sprint (circa otto giorni su dieci in uno sprint di due settimane). Il daily scrum avviene durante l'esecuzione, e la release non coincide con la fine dello sprint."],
["b20","What did Rubin's row-at-a-time versus column-at-a-time exercise show?",["Multitasking took about twice as long and produced more errors","Multitasking was faster, because it kept people's hands busy","Both approaches took the same time and produced the same errors","Single tasking was slower, but it produced fewer errors"],"Figura 20.3: riga per riga (multitasking) circa 35 secondi, colonna per colonna circa 16, e gli errori compaiono nella versione multitasking. Quindi il multitasking non è più veloce né uguale: costa tempo e qualità anche su un compito banale."],
["b20","What is swarming?",["Members with free capacity help finish items already started, before starting new ones","All members start their own item at once, to keep everyone 100% busy","The whole team always works on exactly one product backlog item at a time","The team splits into pairs, and each pair owns a separate feature until release"],"Lo swarming serve a restare concentrati sul goal, non a tenere tutti occupati. Non significa per forza un solo elemento alla volta: il numero giusto dipende da lavoro e competenze."],
["b20","Why does Rubin call a mini waterfall inside the sprint a bad idea?",["If time runs out, every feature may be 90% complete but none done, so the PO gets no value","Because analysis and design activities are forbidden inside a Scrum sprint","Because the testers would have nothing at all to do during the whole first half of the sprint","Because the daily scrum cannot be held while the team is in a waterfall phase"],"Analizzare tutto, poi progettare tutto, poi codificare e testare tutto è rischioso: se manca tempo per i test non c'è nulla di potenzialmente rilasciabile."],
["b20","Who decides the task-level work needed to complete a product backlog item?",["The team, within boundaries such as acceptance criteria and the DoD agreed with the PO","The product owner, who writes the full list of tasks for each item before the sprint","The functional managers of each discipline that is involved in the item","The ScrumMaster, who breaks each item into tasks at the start of the sprint"],"Il lavoro a livello di task lo decide il team; PO e manager lo influenzano con scope, criteri di accettazione e requisiti di business nella definition of done, e i compromessi economici si discutono insieme. Il PO non scrive la lista dei task, e nemmeno ScrumMaster o manager funzionali."],
["b20","Which technical practices does Rubin consider essential for Scrum teams building software?",["Continuous integration, automated testing, refactoring, test-driven development","Detailed design documents, code freezes and long manual regression testing phases","Big up-front architecture, change control boards, phase sign-off gates","Separate QA teams, hardening sprints, end-of-release integration"],"Molte sono note come pratiche di Extreme Programming, ma esistono da decenni. Senza automatizzare i test, la regressione manuale finisce per occupare tutto lo sprint."],
["b20","What do the sprint backlog and the sprint burndown chart track?",["The estimated effort remaining, not the actual effort spent","The actual hours that each person spent on tasks during each day","The story points completed by each developer","The percentage of completion of each task"],"Si aggiorna ogni giorno lo sforzo stimato rimanente. Gli actual non servono a Scrum; l'organizzazione può raccoglierli per altri motivi (contabilità, tasse)."],
["b20","On day 6 the team discovers a missing task for an item it committed to. What should it do?",["Add the task to the sprint backlog: it is real work needed for a committed item","Leave it out, since the sprint backlog is frozen as soon as sprint planning is over","Ask the product owner to add a new item to the product backlog","Drop the item and plan the missing task for the next sprint"],"Aggiungere task per elementi già impegnati non è un modo per far entrare lavoro nuovo: riconosce che in planning non si può prevedere tutto."],
["b20","On a sprint burndown chart, the trend line hits zero well after the end of the sprint. What does this suggest?",["The team is behind the expected pace, or took on too much work","The team is ahead and can safely take on additional work","The team is on time and nothing needs to be investigated","The chart must be reset to the original estimate of each task"],"Se la linea di tendenza arriva a zero ben dopo la fine dello sprint (Late), il team va più piano del previsto o ha preso troppo lavoro: bisogna capire cosa c'è dietro i dati. In anticipo (Early) sarebbe il caso opposto; il grafico non si resetta."],
["b20","Why do many people prefer story points on a sprint burnup chart?",["At sprint end, what matters is completed business-valuable work, measured in points","Story points are the only unit allowed on charts by the Scrum framework","Effort-hours cannot be plotted on the vertical axis of a burnup chart","Story points make the chart look steeper and more convincing to the stakeholders who read it"],"Il burnup in punti mostra a colpo d'occhio come fluiscono i PBI completati; il burndown usa le ore di sforzo rimanenti."],
// ---------- Chapter 21: Sprint Review ----------
["b21","What is the primary purpose of the sprint review?",["Inspect the increment with stakeholders and adapt the backlog and plans from their feedback","Obtain the formal sign-off so that the completed work can be released to the customers right away","Forecast the team's velocity for the next sprint, based on the completed items","Evaluate how well each team member followed the agreed process during the sprint"],"La review serve a ispezionare l'incremento con gli stakeholder e adattare backlog e piani in base al loro feedback: è inspect-and-adapt sul prodotto. Non è un sign-off formale, non serve a prevedere la velocity e il processo del team si valuta nella retrospective."],
["b21","Which is the most valuable outcome of a sprint review?",["An updated product backlog and release outlook that reflect feedback and what was learned","An approved velocity figure that the stakeholders will hold the team to next sprint","A list of defects that must all be cleared before the next sprint is allowed to begin at all","A list of process improvements that the team will try out during the next sprint"],"L'esito più utile della review è un product backlog sistemato e un piano di release aggiornato con il feedback e quello che si è imparato. Una velocity «approvata» diventerebbe un obiettivo da gonfiare, i difetti non bloccano lo sprint successivo, e i miglioramenti di processo sono della retrospective."],
["b21","How should a team typically show its work at a sprint review?",["Demonstrate the working increment, so that feedback is based on the real thing","Walk through slides describing the completed features and their test results","Let stakeholders read the requirement documents and sign that each one is met","Show the sprint backlog and the task board, and explain what was completed"],"Un PowerPoint invece del software fa pensare: «hanno davvero finito?». In alcuni contesti (videogiochi) sono gli stakeholder stessi a provare l'incremento."],
["b21","What timebox does Rubin give for the sprint review?",["About one hour per week of sprint length, up to four hours","Fifteen minutes, exactly like the daily scrum","A full day, whatever the sprint length","Two hours per week of sprint length, up to a maximum of eight hours"],"Regola di un'ora per settimana di sprint, al massimo quattro ore: due ore per uno sprint di due settimane. Quindici minuti è il daily scrum, una giornata intera è troppo, e due ore per settimana raddoppierebbero la regola del libro."],
["b21","Which of the four recurring Scrum activities is hardest to schedule, and why?",["The sprint review, because many attendees are outside the Scrum team","The daily scrum, because it must be held every single day","Sprint planning, because it needs the entire product backlog","The retrospective, because it always requires an external, neutral facilitator"],"Conviene fissare l'orario preferito dagli stakeholder chiave (per esempio ogni secondo venerdì alle 14) e organizzare il resto dello sprint attorno, sfruttando la cadenza."],
["b21","Who determines whether the sprint's work is done before the review?",["The product owner, through just-in-time reviews during the sprint","The stakeholders, by voting at the end of the sprint review","The ScrumMaster, by checking the sprint burndown chart","The development team alone, by running its automated tests"],"È il product owner a stabilire se il lavoro è done, rivedendo gli elementi man mano che sono pronti durante lo sprint: se lo vedesse per la prima volta alla review, sarebbe troppo tardi. Gli stakeholder danno feedback, il burndown non dice se un elemento è done, e il team da solo non basta."],
["b21","How much time do most teams spend preparing the review demonstration?",["No more than 30 minutes to an hour per week of sprint length","At least a full day, to build a polished presentation","About two days per sprint, to rehearse the whole demo","None at all: any preparation would make the review dishonest"],"La maggior parte dei team non spende più di 30-60 minuti per settimana di sprint a preparare la review: è informale, a bassa cerimonia e alto valore, niente produzioni hollywoodiane (eccezione: la visita del generale). Ma un po' di preparazione serve: non è disonesta."],
["b21","What are the steps of Rubin's approach to the sprint review?",["Summarize, demonstrate, discuss, adapt","Plan, build, test, release","Present, approve, sign off, close","Gather data, generate insights, decide, close"],"L'approccio di Rubin alla review: sintesi del goal e di cosa è stato fatto, dimostrazione dell'incremento, discussione (senza problem solving profondo), adattamento di backlog e piano di release. Approvare e firmare non sono passi della review; raccogliere dati e insight è la retrospective."],
["b21","A team only built \"glue code\" this sprint and says there is nothing to demo. What does Rubin say?",["That is almost never true: at minimum, the passing tests can be demonstrated","That is correct: such sprints skip the review and go straight to the retrospective","That is correct: the ScrumMaster summarizes the work with a few slides instead","The product owner should cancel the sprint and restart it with real features"],"Se il PO ha accettato elementi tecnici, deve sapere come verificarli; molti team mettono nella definition of ready il saper dimostrare l'elemento alla review."],
["b21","At the review, a senior stakeholder says an item the product owner accepted is not done. What should happen?",["It stays done; a new backlog item captures the requested change for a future sprint","The item is reopened and the sprint extended until the stakeholder agrees","The item is removed from the increment and its points are subtracted from the team's velocity","The stakeholder becomes the product owner of that item from now on"],"Il PO è il punto centrale di leadership del prodotto e la sua autorità non può essere scavalcata. Deve però capire perché si è disallineato dagli stakeholder."],
["b21","Stakeholders attend sprint reviews only sporadically, because other commitments come first. What does Rubin recommend?",["Stop working on lower-priority products until they matter enough for stakeholders to attend","Hold the review without them and send them a written summary of the results by email afterwards","Move the review to the end of the release, when the stakeholders have more time","Ask the ScrumMaster to represent the stakeholders' point of view at every review"],"È un segnale di troppo lavoro in parallelo. Se i prodotti non diventano mai abbastanza importanti, non vale la pena svilupparli. Se il problema è la sfiducia, la cura è consegnare valore ogni sprint."],
// ---------- Chapter 22: Sprint Retrospective ----------
["b22","Which question best frames the focus of the sprint retrospective?",["How can we improve the way we work together and build the product?","Did we deliver everything the product owner asked for this sprint?","Which stakeholders were dissatisfied with the demonstration at the review?","Who is responsible for the items that were not finished in this sprint?"],"La retrospective serve a migliorare processo e collaborazione, non a cercare colpevoli o a ispezionare di nuovo il prodotto."],
["b22","Who normally takes part in a sprint retrospective?",["The development team, the ScrumMaster and the product owner","The development team and the key stakeholders of the product","The development team only, so that the discussion stays candid","The development team and the line managers of its members"],"Partecipa tutto lo Scrum team: development team, ScrumMaster e product owner (importante, per esempio se i PBI arrivano poco pronti). Stakeholder e manager partecipano solo se invitati; escludere il PO lo lascerebbe fuori da problemi che lo riguardano. Se manca la fiducia, lo ScrumMaster lavora prima sulla sicurezza."],
["b22","Which is the best output of a good retrospective?",["A few specific improvement actions, each owned, to try in the next sprint","A complete list of every problem that the team encountered during the sprint","A formal written report on how the sprint went, prepared for management","A revised velocity estimate that the team will use in the next sprint planning"],"Il miglior output è un piccolo numero di azioni concrete, ognuna con un responsabile, da provare nello sprint successivo; le altre idee vanno in un insight backlog. Una lista di tutti i problemi non si riesce a gestire, un report per il management toglie sicurezza, la velocity non è oggetto della retrospective."],
["b22","Should stakeholders and managers attend the retrospective?",["Only if invited by the Scrum team, since members must feel safe to speak","Always, because transparency is one of the core values of Scrum","Never, under any circumstances, even if the team invites them","Only the managers, so that they can approve the improvement actions"],"Molte organizzazioni non hanno ancora la sicurezza necessaria: con estranei presenti il team potrebbe non parlare dei problemi reali."],
["b22","How much time does Rubin usually budget for the retrospective of a two-week sprint?",["About 1.5 hours","About 15 minutes","About 4 hours","About a full day"],"Rubin di solito prevede circa un'ora e mezza per uno sprint di due settimane, di più per sprint più lunghi: è difficile fare una retrospective utile in meno di 60 minuti. Quindici minuti sono il daily scrum, quattro ore o un giorno sono troppo per uno sprint di due settimane."],
["b22","Who facilitates the sprint retrospective?",["Often the ScrumMaster, but any capable member or an outside facilitator can","Always the product owner, as the owner of the team's process","Always a manager, to make sure that the improvement actions actually get funded","Always the most senior developer, who knows the code base best"],"Spesso facilita lo ScrumMaster, ma può farlo qualsiasi membro capace o un facilitatore esterno e neutrale, utile nelle retrospective difficili (o lo ScrumMaster di un altro team). Non è un compito fisso del PO, di un manager o del senior più esperto."],
["b22","What is an emotions seismograph?",["A curve of each participant's feelings or energy over the sprint, drawn under the timeline","A chart of the number of defects that were found on each day of the sprint","A graph of the team's velocity across its last ten sprints, with the trend lines for each release","A ranking of team members according to their mood at each daily scrum"],"Con l'event timeline serve a creare il contesto condiviso: dati oggettivi (cosa è successo) e soggettivi (come ci si è sentiti)."],
["b22","What is silent grouping?",["Clustering insight cards by moving them around without verbal discussion","Voting on insights privately, without showing anyone the results of the vote","Writing insights anonymously before the retrospective begins","Discussing insights in pairs, then reporting back to the group"],"Nel silent grouping le persone raggruppano le card degli insight spostandole senza parlare: è rapido ed efficace per unire idee simili o duplicate. Non è un voto (quello è il dot voting), non è scrittura anonima e non è una discussione a coppie."],
["b22","How does dot voting work in a retrospective?",["Each participant places a few dots (three to five) on the insights they think matter most","The facilitator places the dots on the insights that the managers prefer","Each participant gets a single dot and must place it on the insight that they wrote themselves","Insights are ranked by how many people wrote them, without any voting"],"Si possono mettere tutti i punti su una card o distribuirli. Si sceglie anche in base a dove c'è energia per migliorare, non solo all'importanza."],
["b22","What is the easiest way to follow through on improvement actions?",["Add tasks for them to the next sprint backlog before taking in new features","Keep a separate improvement plan that the managers review once every quarter","Wait for the next retrospective to decide whether they still matter","Let each person work on them in their spare time, after hours"],"Un piano di miglioramento separato finisce sempre subordinato alle feature. Le azioni che non richiedono tempo del team vanno nella lista impedimenti dello ScrumMaster."],
["b22","What does Rubin call retrospectives that are busy but produce nothing actionable?",["\"All fluff, no stuff\" retrospectives","\"Elephant in the room\" retrospectives","\"Blame game\" retrospectives","\"Complaint session\" retrospectives"],"Rubin chiama «all fluff, no stuff» le retrospective piene di attività ma senza azioni concrete; rimedio: un facilitatore esterno esperto. L'elefante nella stanza è un problema evidente che nessuno nomina, il gioco delle colpe e le sessioni di lamentele sono altri problemi distinti."],
// ---------- Chapter 23: The Path Forward ----------
["b23","What is the end state of a Scrum adoption?",["There is none: becoming more agile is a never-ending improvement","Reaching level 5 of an agile maturity model, as in CMMI","Completing the first full release of a product with Scrum","Having every team member certified as a professional Scrum practitioner"],"Non c'è una definition of done per l'adozione: presupporrebbe di non poter più migliorare. Essere agili non è il fine, ma un mezzo per raggiungere gli obiettivi di business."],
["b23","\"Agile is not something you become, it's something you become more of.\" Who said it?",["Mike Cohn","Ken Schwaber","Jeff Sutherland","Kent Beck"],"La frase è di Mike Cohn (2010), ripresa da Rubin per dire che non esiste uno stato finale chiamato «agile»: si diventa sempre un po' più agili, con miglioramento continuo. Schwaber, Sutherland e Beck sono figure fondamentali dell'agile, ma la citazione non è loro."],
["b23","Why shouldn't an organization simply copy another organization's path to Scrum?",["No two organizations are alike: copying may lead to exactly the wrong place","Scrum forbids sharing practices between different organizations","Other organizations' paths always become outdated within a year or two of adoption","Copying works, but only from organizations in the same country"],"Non si può saltare il proprio processo di apprendimento: si guarda cosa hanno fatto gli altri, ma si scopre la propria strada ispezionando e adattando."],
["b23","How does Rubin distinguish a practice from an approach?",["A practice is a core aspect of Scrum; an approach is a team's own way of performing it","A practice is optional for teams; an approach is required by the Scrum framework","A practice belongs to one team; an approach is shared by the whole organization","A practice is technical work; an approach concerns the management of people"],"Tutti devono seguire le stesse pratiche (ruoli, sprint planning, daily scrum, review, retrospective); ogni team ha i suoi approcci. Le «best practice» sono in realtà best approach."],
["b23","What does the \"toss the moose\" story show?",["Approaches are unique to each team and should be reused only if they fit its context","Every team should adopt the best practice of the most successful team in the company","The daily scrum becomes optional when a team finds a fun alternative to it","The ScrumMaster should always decide who speaks first at the daily scrum"],"Il team di Vancouver lanciava un alce di peluche per decidere chi parlava per primo; tradotto in cinese «toss the moose» aveva tutt'altro significato, e il team cinese scelse un suo approccio."],
["b23","What did the Working Software Group, an Enterprise Transition Community, do?",["Managers and executives ran three-week sprints against an improvement backlog","Developers rewrote the code of every team to a single common standard","Consultants wrote a complete transition plan before any team was allowed to start","Testers checked that every team followed exactly the same approaches"],"Il Working Software Group era un Enterprise Transition Community: manager e dirigenti lavoravano in sprint di tre settimane su un backlog di miglioramenti (iniziative di cambiamento e impedimenti dei team). Usare Scrum per adottare Scrum, senza un piano completo calato dall'alto o controlli di conformità."],
["b23","Some people won't start Scrum until every detail of their approach is worked out. What does Rubin say?",["Get going: you can't get it perfect up front, and the next sprint will teach you more","Wait: a complete adoption plan prevents failures in the first sprints","Start only after a pilot team has finished at least one full release","Hire a coach to write down the full approach in detail before running the first sprint"],"È la stessa incoerenza di chi sa che i requisiti non si azzeccano all'inizio ma vuole definire tutto Scrum prima di partire. I primi sprint di solito non sono belli, ed è normale."],
["b23","What does Scrum do with organizational dysfunctions, according to Chapter 23?",["It makes them visible, but solving them is up to the people in the organization","It solves them automatically through the retrospectives of each team","It hides them until the teams are mature enough to deal with them","It ignores them, because Scrum focuses only on the product that is being built by the team"],"Scrum rende visibili disfunzioni e sprechi, ma non dice come risolverli: è un lavoro delle persone dell'organizzazione, contro la forza dello status quo. Non li risolve da solo con le retrospective, non li nasconde e non li ignora."],
["b23","What is Rubin's only expectation of any Scrum team?",["That it be better in the next sprint than in the previous one","That its very first sprints go smoothly, without any problem at all","That its velocity doubles in every release","That it never misses a single sprint goal"],"L'unica aspettativa di Rubin è che ogni team sia migliore nello sprint successivo rispetto al precedente: miglioramento continuo. I primi sprint di solito non sono belli, la velocity non deve raddoppiare e mancare un goal può capitare."],
["b23","In Rubin's 2007 example, how were approaches shared among the pilot Scrum teams?",["Through a wiki that syndicated what each team was doing","Through a mandatory rulebook issued by management","Through a single standard process imposed on all teams","Through monthly audits by an external certification body"],"Nell'esempio del 2007 (100 persone IT a New York, 400 a Mumbai) ogni team pilota aveva la sua versione di Scrum, e un wiki raccoglieva gli approcci di tutti per l'apprendimento dell'organizzazione. Un regolamento obbligatorio o un processo unico imposto andrebbero contro la scoperta del proprio percorso."],
];

const BOOK_MULTI = [
// ---------- Chapter 1: Introduction ----------
["b01","According to Chapter 1, what does Kanban advocate? (choose three)",["Visualize how the work flows through the system","Limit the work in process at each step","Measure and optimize the flow of work"],["Plan the work in fixed-length timeboxed iterations","Assign a product owner to order a product backlog"],"Kanban non è un processo a sé ma si sovrappone a quello esistente: visualizzare il flusso, limitare il WIP a ogni passo, misurare e ottimizzare il flusso."],
["b01","Which of these are Scrum benefits shown in Figure 1.2? (choose three)",["Delighted customers","Improved return on investment","More joy"],["Guaranteed delivery dates","Fewer meetings for the team"],"Figura 1.2: clienti entusiasti, ROI migliore, costi ridotti, risultati veloci, fiducia nel riuscire in un mondo complesso, più gioia. Scrum non garantisce date."],
// ---------- Chapter 2: Scrum Framework ----------
["b02","Which of the following are Scrum artifacts? (choose three)",["Sprint backlog","Potentially shippable product increment","Product backlog"],["Product backlog grooming","Sprint review"],"Artefatti: product backlog, sprint backlog, incremento di prodotto potenzialmente rilasciabile. Grooming e sprint review sono attività."],
["b02","Which statements about the Scrum roles are true? (choose two)",["The development team decides how to turn product backlog items into a potentially shippable increment","The product owner is responsible for what gets built and in what order"],["The product owner decides how many items the development team must take into the sprint","The ScrumMaster assigns tasks to development team members at the start of each sprint"],"Il product owner decide cosa e in che ordine; il team decide come e quanto lavoro può prendere. Lo ScrumMaster non assegna task."],
["b02","Which of the following are activities in Rubin's Scrum framework? (choose three)",["Sprint planning","Daily scrum","Product backlog grooming"],["Release hardening","Requirements sign-off"],"Attività: grooming, sprint planning, sprint execution, daily scrum, sprint review, sprint retrospective. Hardening e sign-off non fanno parte del framework."],
// ---------- Chapter 3: Agile Principles ----------
["b03","Which of the following are value statements in the Agile Manifesto (the first item valued more than the second)? (choose two)",["Individuals and interactions over processes and tools","Working software over comprehensive documentation"],["Following a plan over responding to change","Customer collaboration over requirements specification"],"Il Manifesto dice responding to change over following a plan, e customer collaboration over contract negotiation (non requirements specification)."],
["b03","Why do Scrum and lean thinking favor limiting work in process? (choose two)",["Items reach done sooner, shortening the time from start to feedback","Less partially done work exists, so less is wasted if priorities change"],["The product backlog no longer needs to be prioritized when less work is open","Every team member can be kept 100% busy on their own specialty"],"Meno WIP significa tempi di attraversamento più brevi, feedback prima e meno inventario a rischio. Non serve a massimizzare l'utilizzo delle persone."],
["b03","Which principles belong to the Performance category of Rubin's agile principles? (choose three)",["Go fast but never hurry","Build in quality","Employ minimally sufficient ceremony"],["Keep options open","Consider cost of delay"],"Performance: go fast but never hurry, build in quality, minimally sufficient ceremony. Keep options open è in Prediction and adaptation, cost of delay in Work in process."],
// ---------- Chapter 4: Sprints ----------
["b04","Which statements are consistent with how Scrum treats sprints? (choose two)",["Keeping the sprint length constant helps the team establish a predictable cadence","Sprints are timeboxed: if work is not done, the end date does not move"],["A new sprint starts only after the product owner formally approves the previous one","The team may lengthen a sprint by a few days when it is close to the sprint goal"],"Gli sprint hanno durata costante, che dà una cadenza prevedibile, e sono timeboxed: se il lavoro non è finito, la data di fine non si sposta. Il successivo parte subito dopo la fine del precedente, senza attendere approvazioni, e lo sprint non si allunga nemmeno se il goal è vicino."],
["b04","The definition of done can evolve over time. Which examples from the book illustrate this? (choose two)",["A team adds clinical testing to its DoD once it gets access to a clinical lab","A hardware-and-software team strengthens its DoD once the real hardware arrives"],["A team removes testing from its DoD so that it can finish more features each sprint","A product owner relaxes the DoD for the items that stakeholders liked at the review"],"La DoD evolve man mano che si rimuovono impedimenti (accesso a un laboratorio clinico, hardware disponibile), verso il potenzialmente rilasciabile. Non si allenta per fare più feature."],
// ---------- Chapter 5: Requirements and User Stories ----------
["b05","Which are correct pairings of an INVEST letter with its meaning? (choose two)",["N: Negotiable","V: Valuable"],["T: Traceable","S: Simple"],"INVEST (Bill Wake): Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimatable, Sized appropriately (Small), Testable. T sta per Testable, non Traceable; S per Small, non Simple."],
["b05","Which statements about epics and themes are true? (choose two)",["An epic is a large story, months in size, that is split before it is scheduled","A theme is a collection of related user stories"],["Epics should be sized so that several of them fit within a single sprint","A theme must be fully specified before its stories enter the product backlog"],"Epic: storia grande (mesi), un segnaposto che si scompone più avanti. Theme: un gruppo di storie correlate, come un mazzo di card tenute insieme da un elastico."],
["b05","Which names does Rubin give for knowledge-acquisition work? (choose three)",["Spike","Proof of concept","Prototype"],["Hardening sprint","Technical debt"],"Prototipo, proof of concept, esperimento, studio e spike sono nomi del lavoro di acquisizione di conoscenza, che serve a comprare informazione. Uno sprint di hardening serve a stabilizzare prima del rilascio, e il debito tecnico è un'altra cosa."],
// ---------- Chapter 6: Product Backlog ----------
["b06","Which of the following can be product backlog items? (choose three)",["Features","Defects","Knowledge-acquisition work"],["Developer tasks such as \"write unit tests for class X\"","The hours each developer logged last sprint"],"Sono PBI feature, modifiche, difetti, miglioramenti tecnici e lavoro di acquisizione di conoscenza. I task fini come «scrivere gli unit test della classe X» stanno nello sprint backlog, e le ore registrate non sono lavoro da ordinare."],
["b06","Which factors should influence how product backlog items are prioritized? (choose three)",["The value the item delivers","Knowledge the team would gain by building it","Risk and uncertainty associated with the item"],["The seniority of the person requesting it","The order in which the requests were submitted"],"Valore, conoscenza, incertezza (oltre a costo e dipendenze) guidano l'ordine. Chi chiede e quando l'ha chiesto non sono criteri."],
["b06","Which items appear in Rubin's example definition-of-ready checklist? (choose two)",["Business value is clearly articulated","The Scrum team understands how to demonstrate the PBI at the sprint review"],["The PBI has been approved by the change control board","Every task of the PBI has been assigned to a developer"],"Tabella 6.2: valore chiaro, dettagli capiti, dipendenze identificate, team adeguato, PBI stimato e piccolo, criteri di accettazione e di prestazione testabili, il team sa come mostrarlo alla review. Un comitato di change control e l'assegnazione dei task alle persone non fanno parte di Scrum."],
// ---------- Chapter 7: Estimation and Velocity ----------
["b07","Which statements about velocity are true? (choose two)",["Velocity is the sum of the sizes of items completed to the definition of done in a sprint","Velocity is only meaningful if items are sized consistently from sprint to sprint"],["A story that is 90% complete at sprint end contributes 90% of its points","Velocity can be used to compare the productivity of different teams that both use points"],"Contano solo gli elementi completati secondo la DoD. Le scale di punti cambiano da team a team, quindi le velocity non si confrontano."],
["b07","Which are PBI estimation concepts described in Chapter 7? (choose three)",["Estimate as a team","Estimates are not commitments","Use relative rather than absolute sizes"],["Estimate in elapsed calendar days","Let the most senior person decide"],"Concetti: stimare come team, le stime non sono impegni, accuratezza più che precisione, dimensioni relative invece che assolute."],
// ---------- Chapter 8: Technical Debt ----------
["b08","Which are likely effects of accumulating technical debt? (choose two)",["Velocity tends to decline as changes become more expensive","Forecasts become less predictable because the cost of a change is harder to know"],["Time to market keeps improving in the long run because shortcuts were taken early","The cost of changing the affected code falls as workarounds accumulate"],"Il debito fa scendere la velocity, perché ogni cambiamento costa di più, e rende le previsioni meno affidabili. Le scorciatoie possono aiutare nel breve, ma nel lungo periodo il time to market peggiora e il costo di cambiare il codice cresce, non scende."],
["b08","Which approaches are consistent with managing technical debt in Scrum? (choose two)",["Making significant debt visible, for example with product backlog items for paying it down","Building small clean-ups and refactoring into normal work, leaving code cleaner than found"],["Deferring all debt repayment until after the final release has shipped","Keeping the debt hidden from the product owner so that priorities are not disrupted"],"Il debito va reso visibile (per esempio con elementi del backlog per ripagarlo) e ripagato con piccole pulizie e refactoring nel lavoro normale. Rimandare tutto a dopo il rilascio lo fa crescere fino al punto di non ritorno, nasconderlo al PO impedisce scelte economiche."],
["b08","In which situations does Rubin say technical debt should not be repaid? (choose three)",["Product nearing end of life","Throwaway prototype","Product built for a short life"],["Debt in a frequently modified core module","Debt found while working on a new feature"],"Non tutto il debito va ripagato: per un prodotto a fine vita, un prototipo usa e getta o un prodotto pensato per durare poco, ripagarlo è uno spreco."],
// ---------- Chapter 9: Product Owner ----------
["b09","Which statements are consistent with the product owner role? (choose two)",["The product owner is empowered to make product decisions without constantly seeking approval","The product owner is a single person, even when many stakeholders provide input"],["The product owner may also act as ScrumMaster for the same team to reduce overhead","The product owner decides how the development team implements each item"],"Una sola voce con potere decisionale mantiene chiara la direzione. Unire PO e ScrumMaster è sconsigliato e l'implementazione spetta al team."],
["b09","Which are principal product owner responsibilities? (choose three)",["Manage economics","Groom the product backlog","Define acceptance criteria and verify they are met"],["Assign tasks to developers","Facilitate the daily scrum"],"Responsabilità del PO (figura 9.2): gestire l'economia (release, sprint, backlog), partecipare alla pianificazione, fare grooming, definire e verificare i criteri di accettazione, collaborare con team e stakeholder. I task li sceglie il team, il daily scrum è del team, facilitato dallo ScrumMaster."],
// ---------- Chapter 10: ScrumMaster ----------
["b10","Which are typical ScrumMaster responsibilities? (choose two)",["Helping the team resolve or escalate impediments","Facilitating Scrum activities when asked or needed"],["Evaluating individual developers for performance reviews","Deciding which product backlog items the team commits to"],"Facilitazione e rimozione degli impedimenti sono centrali. L'impegno lo decide il team e le valutazioni delle persone non spettano allo ScrumMaster."],
["b10","Which statements about the ScrumMaster role are true according to Rubin? (choose two)",["Every Scrum team has a ScrumMaster, but the role may not always be full-time","A functional manager can be a good ScrumMaster, ideally without managing the team's members"],["The ScrumMaster is accountable for making sure that the committed work gets done","The team's dev lead should always be the ScrumMaster, to save one role"],"Il ruolo può non essere a tempo pieno con un team maturo. Un manager può farlo, ma è meglio che i membri del team non riportino a lui."],
// ---------- Chapter 11: Development Team ----------
["b11","Which are characteristics of a well-formed development team? (choose two)",["The team is self-organizing and decides how to do its work","Members have T-shaped skills: deep in one area and broad in others"],["Membership rotates every sprint so that knowledge is shared","Job titles restrict who on the team may do testing work"],"Auto-organizzazione e competenze a T sono centrali in un buon team. Ruotare i membri a ogni sprint distrugge fiducia e storico, e titoli rigidi su chi può testare impediscono lo swarming."],
["b11","Which reasons for keeping teams small does Rubin cite from Cohn? (choose three)",["Less social loafing","Less time spent coordinating efforts","No one can fade into the background"],["More specialization of each member","Easier to keep everyone 100% busy"],"Le ragioni di Cohn per i team piccoli: meno social loafing, più interazione costruttiva, meno coordinamento, nessuno si nasconde, più soddisfazione, meno specializzazione dannosa. Più specializzazione e persone occupate al 100% non sono vantaggi per Rubin, anzi."],
// ---------- Chapter 12: Scrum Team Structures ----------
["b12","Which statements about release-train planning are true? (choose two)",["The chief product owner leads it and gives the big picture for the PSI","Teams slot features into specific sprints (sprint mapping) and negotiate dependencies"],["Each team plans alone, without any contact with the other teams","The ScrumMasters decide which features go into each team's sprints"],"Grande stanza, centinaia di persone: il chief PO introduce, i PO di feature area spiegano le loro aree, i team fanno sprint mapping e si accordano sulle dipendenze. Alla fine: PSI review e retrospective del treno."],
// ---------- Chapter 13: Managers ----------
["b13","Which are ways managers nurture teams, according to Chapter 13? (choose three)",["Energize people","Develop competence","Maintain team integrity"],["Assign task-level work","Set the sprint goals"],"I manager fanno crescere i team energizzando le persone, sviluppando le competenze, guidando la propria area funzionale e mantenendo l'integrità del team. Assegnare task toglie energia e mina l'auto-organizzazione, e lo sprint goal lo concordano PO e team."],
// ---------- Chapter 14: Scrum Planning Principles ----------
["b14","Which are consistent with Scrum planning principles? (choose two)",["Plan at multiple levels, each with its own purpose and level of detail","Treat plans as hypotheses to be inspected and adapted as the team learns"],["Fix scope, schedule and cost at the start, so that the plan can be baselined","Estimate all product backlog items in hours up front, for maximum precision"],"Pianificare a più livelli, ognuno con il suo scopo e dettaglio, e trattare i piani come ipotesi da ispezionare e adattare. Fissare scope, tempi e costi all'inizio e stimare tutto in ore contraddicono l'approccio adattivo."],
["b14","Which are Scrum planning principles listed in Chapter 14? (choose three)",["Keep planning options open until the last responsible moment","Correctly manage the planning inventory","Favor smaller and more frequent releases"],["Baseline the plan and control every change","Plan everything up front to reduce risk"],"Principi: non pensare di azzeccare i piani all'inizio; pianificazione up-front utile ma non eccessiva; opzioni aperte fino al last responsible moment; adattare più che conformarsi; gestire l'inventario di pianificazione; rilasci piccoli e frequenti; imparare in fretta e fare pivot."],
// ---------- Chapter 15: Multilevel Planning ----------
["b15","Which statements correctly match a planning level to its focus? (choose two)",["Portfolio planning decides which products to work on, in what order and for how long","At the daily scrum the team plans its next 24 hours of work toward the sprint goal"],["Product planning (vision and roadmap) defines the detailed tasks for the next sprint","Release planning assigns specific tasks to the individual developers of each team"],"Il portfolio planning decide su quali prodotti lavorare, in che ordine e per quanto; al daily scrum il team pianifica le prossime 24 ore verso lo sprint goal. La pianificazione dei task è a livello di sprint e di giorno, non di product planning o release planning, e nessuno assegna task alle persone."],
// ---------- Chapter 16: Portfolio Planning ----------
["b16","Which are inflow strategies in Rubin's portfolio planning? (choose three)",["Apply the economic filter","Quickly embrace emergent opportunities","Plan for smaller, more frequent releases"],["Wait for a complete team","Use marginal economics"],"Inflow: filtro economico, bilanciare arrivi e partenze, cogliere le opportunità emergenti, rilasci piccoli. Outflow: idle work, WIP limit, team completo. In-process: marginal economics."],
// ---------- Chapter 17: Envisioning (Product Planning) ----------
["b17","Which are guidelines for economically sensible envisioning? (choose three)",["Target a realistic confidence threshold","Focus on a short horizon","Learn fast and pivot (aka fail fast)"],["Plan the full vision before funding anything","Wait for complete market certainty"],"Figura 17.7: soglia di confidenza realistica, orizzonte breve, agire in fretta, pagare per il validated learning, finanziamento incrementale, imparare in fretta e fare pivot. Pianificare tutta la visione prima di finanziare o aspettare la certezza producono solo un'illusione di certezza e ritardi."],
// ---------- Chapter 19: Sprint Planning ----------
["b19","Which are outputs of sprint planning? (choose two)",["A sprint goal","A sprint backlog"],["A potentially shippable product increment","Final acceptance of the sprint's items by the product owner"],"Lo sprint planning produce lo sprint goal e lo sprint backlog (elementi scelti più il piano per realizzarli). L'incremento potenzialmente rilasciabile è il risultato dell'esecuzione dello sprint, e l'accettazione degli elementi avviene durante lo sprint e prima della review."],
["b19","Which factors affect the team's capacity for a sprint? (choose two)",["Planned vacations and public holidays of team members","Time the team expects to spend on grooming and other Scrum activities"],["The total number of points remaining in the product backlog","The product owner's availability for the review of the previous sprint"],"Capacità: tempo per le attività di Scrum, impegni fuori dallo sprint, ferie, più un buffer per gli imprevisti. Il backlog rimanente non c'entra."],
["b19","Which are inputs to sprint planning in Table 19.1? (choose three)",["A product backlog groomed into a ready state","The team's velocity","The initial sprint goal"],["The task assignments of each developer","The burndown charts of other teams"],"Input dello sprint planning (tabella 19.1): product backlog pronto, velocity, vincoli, capacità del team (chi c'è, competenze, disponibilità), sprint goal iniziale. I task non si assegnano in anticipo alle persone, e i burndown di altri team non servono."],
// ---------- Chapter 20: Sprint Execution ----------
["b20","Which are sound practices for the daily scrum? (choose two)",["Timebox it, typically to 15 minutes","Hold it at the same time and place each day"],["Have the ScrumMaster run it and collect each person's status","Resolve every impediment in detail during the meeting, with everyone present"],"Timebox breve (di solito 15 minuti) e stessa ora e luogo ogni giorno. Il daily scrum lo conduce il team, non è uno status per lo ScrumMaster, e i problemi si risolvono dopo tra le persone coinvolte."],
// ---------- Chapter 21: Sprint Review ----------
["b21","Which are appropriate in a sprint review? (choose two)",["Collecting stakeholder feedback that may change the product backlog","Demonstrating the items that meet the definition of done"],["Showing nearly finished items so that the team gets credit for effort","Assessing the performance of individual developers"],"Si mostra solo lavoro done, così il feedback riguarda funzionalità reali e complete. La review riguarda il prodotto, non le persone."],
["b21","Which are benefits of a joint sprint review for several interrelated teams? (choose two)",["Stakeholders attend one review instead of several","The review can focus on the integrated work"],["It is always shorter than a single-team review","It removes the need for integration testing"],"Con una review congiunta gli stakeholder partecipano a una review sola invece che a molte, e si guarda il lavoro integrato. Per farlo le DoD di tutti i team devono includere i test di integrazione; non è più breve, anzi dura di più e serve una stanza più grande."],
// ---------- Chapter 22: Sprint Retrospective ----------
["b22","Which steps belong to Rubin's approach to the sprint retrospective? (choose three)",["Set the atmosphere","Identify insights","Determine actions"],["Re-estimate the product backlog","Assign blame for missed items"],"Approccio di Rubin (simile a Derby e Larsen): set the atmosphere, share context, identify insights, determine actions, close the retrospective; poi il follow-through. Ristimare il backlog è grooming, e cercare colpevoli va contro una retrospective senza colpe."],
["b22","Which are common sprint retrospective issues listed by Rubin? (choose three)",["Ignoring the elephant in the room","Blame game and finger-pointing","No follow-through on the actions"],["Using objective data to build a shared context","Closing with appreciation for the participants"],"Figura 22.12: retrospective saltate o poco frequentate, elefante nella stanza, gioco delle colpe, deprimenti, sostituire il miglioramento continuo ad hoc, ambizioni eccessive, nessun seguito alle azioni. Usare dati oggettivi e chiudere con apprezzamenti sono buone pratiche."],
];

const BOOK_OPEN = [
// ---------- Chapter 1: Introduction ----------
["b01","Using the Cynefin framework, explain when Scrum is and is not the best choice, and what fits better in each case.",["Complex: Scrum particularly well suited (probe, sense, respond)","Complicated: experts and good practice; Scrum works but may not be best (performance tuning, maintenance)","Simple: best practices, a repeatable procedure is more efficient","Chaotic: act immediately, someone takes charge","Disorder: break the situation into parts and move each to another domain","Interrupt-driven work (support): Kanban"]],
["b01","Summarize the history of Scrum and the reasons that led Genomica to adopt it.",["1986 Takeuchi and Nonaka: rugby approach vs relay race","1993 Sutherland at Easel: combined with OO, empirical process control, iterative-incremental, complex adaptive systems","1995 Schwaber, first paper at OOPSLA","Genomica: previous approach failing, complex domain, need for fast feedback with a partner","Avoid big up-front architecture (42 seconds per field), cross-functional teams, daily sync"]],
// ---------- Chapter 2: Scrum Framework ----------
["b02","Walk through a single sprint and explain how the three roles, the main activities and the artifacts of Scrum interact. Where do inspection and adaptation take place?",["Product owner -> product backlog; team -> sprint backlog; output -> potentially shippable increment","Sprint planning, sprint execution (daily scrum, grooming), sprint review, sprint retrospective in order","Review = inspect/adapt the product; retrospective = inspect/adapt the process","Daily scrum is a smaller inspect-and-adapt loop toward the sprint goal","ScrumMaster's facilitation and coaching role"]],
// ---------- Chapter 3: Agile Principles ----------
["b03","Explain the idea of validated learning and describe how Scrum's sprint structure supports it.",["Assumption: a belief not yet validated; validated learning: knowledge that confirms or refutes it","Validate important assumptions fast, so mistakes are found early and cheaply","Multiple concurrent learning loops: daily scrum, sprint review, retrospective; pair programming and TDD within minutes","Organize workflow for fast feedback: problems are found before they compound and bad paths are cut short","The product backlog and the plans are updated with what is learned"]],
["b03","Compare plan-driven and agile principles on at least four topics of Table 3.4.",["Process: phase-based and sequential vs iterative and incremental","Uncertainty: end first then means vs all simultaneously","Decisions: in their phase vs keep options open (last responsible moment)","Batch size: all before any vs small economic batches; WIP and idle work","Progress: phases vs validated working assets; quality at the end vs built in","Ceremony: high formality vs minimally sufficient"]],
// ---------- Chapter 4: Sprints ----------
["b04","How should a team decide on its sprint length, and why should the length usually remain constant once chosen?",["Sprints last from one week to one calendar month and are timeboxed","Short duration: ease of planning, fast feedback, better ROI, bounded error, rejuvenated excitement, frequent checkpoints","Pick a length that gives feedback often enough for the uncertainty involved","Consistent length: cadence (rhythm, less coordination, synchronized teams) and simpler planning (comparable velocity)","Change it only for compelling reasons (holidays, a release date), never because the work did not fit"]],
// ---------- Chapter 5: Requirements and User Stories ----------
["b05","Write a well-formed user story for a customer paying with a saved credit card, add two acceptance criteria, and explain how the story supports conversation rather than replacing it.",["Role, goal, benefit: As a returning customer, I want to pay with my saved credit card, so that I can check out quickly","Acceptance criteria specific and testable, e.g. an expired card shows an error; the security code is still required","Three Cs: the card is a reminder, the conversation adds the details, the confirmation is the criteria","Details are clarified with the product owner through conversation, just in time","Check INVEST: valuable, small enough for a sprint, testable"]],
["b05","Explain when a knowledge-acquisition story is worth doing, using an economic argument.",["Exploration buys information (prototype, spike, proof of concept)","Bound it: the team sizes the story, so its cost is known","Estimate the value: probability of being wrong times the cost of being wrong","Do it only if the value of the information exceeds its cost; otherwise guess and fail fast","Deliverable: a short memo with experiments, results, recommendation"]],
// ---------- Chapter 6: Product Backlog ----------
["b06","Describe product backlog grooming: what activities it includes, who is involved, when it happens and why it matters.",["Activities: creating and refining, estimating and prioritizing items; keeps the backlog DEEP","The product owner leads; stakeholders, ScrumMaster and development team collaborate; the team spends up to 10% of its time","When: initial grooming in release planning, then ongoing (weekly or once-a-sprint workshops, the sprint review)","Keeps the top items ready (definition of ready), about two to three sprints' worth","Supports release flow (must have, nice to have, won't have) and sprint flow"]],
["b06","How many product backlogs should an organization have? Discuss large products, multiple teams and one team with multiple products.",["One product, one product backlog (a product is something a customer would pay for)","Large products: hierarchical backlogs with a chief product owner","Multiple teams, one backlog; team-specific views if teams are not interchangeable","One team, multiple products: one product per sprint, or merge the backlogs with a single prioritization","Avoid multi-projecting"]],
// ---------- Chapter 7: Estimation and Velocity ----------
["b07","Explain why velocity should not be used to compare teams, and describe two ways velocity can be misused.",["Teams use different point scales and definitions of done (a 5 for one team can be a 50 for another)","Rewarding high velocity causes point inflation","Pressure on velocity leads to cut corners, technical debt and burnout from overtime","Velocity measures output, not outcome","Use it as a range for the team's own planning and as an internal diagnostic"]],
// ---------- Chapter 8: Technical Debt ----------
["b08","Explain the technical debt metaphor, name two common causes of technical debt, and describe how a Scrum team can both prevent and reduce it.",["Metaphor: a shortcut is a loan; the interest is the extra effort paid every time; the principal is the cost of fixing it","Types: naive, unavoidable, strategic","Causes: pressure to meet a deadline, attempting to falsely accelerate velocity, the myth that less testing is faster; debt builds on debt","Prevent: good technical practices (CI, TDD, refactoring, automated tests), a strong definition of done, understanding its economics","Reduce: make it visible (business and technical level), service it incrementally, high-interest first"]],
["b08","Describe how a Scrum team should service technical debt (categories and algorithm).",["Categories: happened-upon, known, targeted","Decide whether the debt should be serviced at all (end of life, throwaway prototype, short life)","Boy Scout rule for happened-upon debt; log the rest as known debt","Each sprint target some known debt, high-interest first","Repay while doing customer-valuable work, not in dedicated sprints"]],
// ---------- Chapter 9: Product Owner ----------
["b09","Describe what a product owner does before, during and after a sprint, and name two tensions the role must manage.",["Faces two directions: the voice of the stakeholders, and tells the team what to build and in what order","Before: envisioning and release planning, grooming, the initial sprint goal at sprint planning","During: available every day, answers questions, reviews items and verifies acceptance criteria just in time, keeps grooming","After: sprint review (summary, feedback), updates the backlog and release plan, joins the retrospective","Manages economics at release, sprint and backlog level (would I write a check from my own account for this sprint?)"]],
["b09","Who should be the product owner in different kinds of development (internal, commercial, outsourced, component), and how is the role scaled?",["Internal: empowered business representative of the group that benefits","Commercial: internal proxy for customers, e.g. product manager","Outsourced: someone from the company that pays; component team: a technical person","Proxy for tactical decisions, but a single ultimate decision maker","Large products: chief product owner with a hierarchy of product owners"]],
// ---------- Chapter 10: ScrumMaster ----------
["b10","Compare the ScrumMaster with a traditional project manager in terms of authority, accountability and how each deals with impediments.",["Project manager: command and control, plans and assigns tasks, accountable for adherence to the plan","ScrumMaster: servant leader and coach; process authority without managerial authority (no hiring, firing or task assignment)","The ScrumMaster is not accountable for getting the work done: the team self-organizes","Impediments: the team solves what it can; the ScrumMaster removes the rest, working with whoever can fix them","The project manager's responsibilities are distributed among product owner, ScrumMaster, team and managers"]],
["b10","Describe a day in the life of a ScrumMaster during a sprint (Figure 10.3).",["Organizing and facilitating Scrum activities","Coaching the team on Scrum and technical practices, refresher training","Assisting the product owner with grooming and economic trade-offs","Communicating (burndown/burnup charts, people outside the team)","Change agent across the value chain","Impediment removal: variable, can reshape the whole day"]],
// ---------- Chapter 11: Development Team ----------
["b11","Explain what cross-functional means for a Scrum development team and how a team can handle a gap in a specialized skill without breaking this principle.",["Cross-functionally diverse and sufficient: all the skills to deliver done items are in the team","Role-specific teams (e.g. a separate testing team) create handoffs and are suspect; testing is part of every sprint","T-shaped skills let members swarm on bottlenecks","A scarce specialist can be shared among a reasonable number of teams, not too many","Specialists help the others learn their skills, so the team depends less on them"]],
["b11","List and briefly explain the characteristics Rubin wants in a development team.",["Self-organizing; cross-functionally diverse and sufficient; T-shaped skills","Musketeer attitude; high-bandwidth and transparent communication","Right-sized (5-9; N(N-1)/2 channels)","Focused and committed (one or two products at most)","Sustainable pace; long-lived (the team is the asset and the unit of capacity)"]],
// ---------- Chapter 12: Scrum Team Structures ----------
["b12","Compare feature teams and component teams, and describe the blended model Rubin suggests.",["Feature team: cross-functional and cross-component, completes end-customer features","Component team: one component area, delivers only pieces; multiplies delay risk","Why organizations like component teams: one trusted team per component","Blended model: feature teams plus component teams; pollinator and harvester","Goal: mostly feature teams with shared code ownership"]],
["b12","Describe two techniques for coordinating multiple Scrum teams.",["Scrum of scrums: representatives chosen by each team, a few times a week","Three inter-team questions; 15-minute timebox or extended for problem solving; can be multilevel","Release train: common cadence, fixed dates, train waits for no one","Aligned sprints of equal length, PSI every 60-120 days, possible hardening sprint","Joint release planning led by the chief product owner, sprint mapping, PSI review and retrospective"]],
// ---------- Chapter 13: Managers ----------
["b13","What is the role of functional managers in a Scrum organization? Compare it with the traditional role (Table 13.4).",["Fashion teams: boundaries (sandboxes), clear elevating goal, form and change teams, empower (Appelo's levels)","Nurture teams: energize, develop competence, frequent feedback, functional-area leadership, team integrity","Align and adapt the environment: agile values, remove impediments, align internal groups and partners","Manage value-creation flow: systems perspective, economics, measures aligned with agile (cycle time)","Traditional vs Scrum: assign tasks vs let teams self-organize; move people vs keep team integrity"]],
["b13","Where is the project manager in Scrum?",["PMI responsibilities (scope, time, cost, quality, team, communications, risk...) still matter","Distributed among product owner, ScrumMaster, development team and other managers (Table 13.3)","Project managers may become ScrumMasters, product owners or team members","Separate role only at large scale or multiparty efforts, as servant coordinator","Teams still own their coordination"]],
// ---------- Chapter 14: Scrum Planning Principles ----------
["b14","Explain why Scrum plans at multiple levels with differing detail, and give one example of progressive refinement.",["We can't get the plans right up front; up-front planning should be helpful without being excessive","Plan at multiple levels: each decision needs its own horizon and level of detail","Keep planning options open until the last responsible moment; avoid excessive planning inventory","Add detail just in time as knowledge grows; adapt and replan rather than conform","Example: epic → feature → sprintable stories → tasks at sprint planning"]],
["b14","Explain, with numbers, why Scrum favors smaller and more frequent releases.",["Single release: costs paid long before revenue; payback, self-funding and breakeven come late","Incremental releases reach self-funding, breakeven and profit sooner","Table 14.2: IRR 9.1% single, 15.7% semiannual, 19.5% quarterly; less cash invested","Limit: minimum releasable features; some markets can't absorb frequent releases"]],
// ---------- Chapter 15: Multilevel Planning ----------
["b15","Describe the levels of the planning onion. For each level give a typical horizon, the main participants and the key output.",["Portfolio: possibly a year or more; stakeholders and product owners; portfolio backlog and in-process products","Product (envisioning): many months or longer; product owner and stakeholders; vision, roadmap, high-level features","Release: three (or fewer) to nine months; Scrum team and stakeholders; release plan","Sprint: one week to a month; Scrum team; sprint goal and sprint backlog","Daily: every day; ScrumMaster and development team; the plan of the day. Moving inward the horizon shrinks and detail grows"]],
// ---------- Chapter 16: Portfolio Planning ----------
["b16","Describe the strategies Rubin proposes for portfolio planning, grouped by category.",["Scheduling: lifecycle profits; cost of delay (WSJF); accuracy not precision (T-shirt sizes)","Inflow: economic filter; balance arrival and departure (monthly); emergent opportunities; smaller releases (convoy effect)","Outflow: idle work not idle workers; WIP limit with teams as unit of capacity; wait for a complete team","In-process: marginal economics, sunk cost; preserve, pivot, deliver, terminate"]],
["b16","What is the cost of delay, how is it used, and which delay profiles exist?",["Cost to lifecycle profits of delaying a product, e.g. by one month","Used to sequence the portfolio; WSJF = cost of delay / duration","Profiles: linear, large fixed cost, must do now, fixed date, logarithmic, intangible","Applies even in regulated environments (cost of non-compliance)"]],
// ---------- Chapter 17: Envisioning (Product Planning) ----------
["b17","Describe the envisioning activity: timing, participants, inputs and outputs, using SR4U as an example.",["Ongoing; idea passes the strategic filter; output goes to portfolio planning (economic filter)","Product owner is the only required participant; stakeholders and specialists often join","Inputs: planning horizon, completion date, budget/resources, confidence threshold","Outputs: vision (e.g. press release), high-level product backlog (epics), roadmap, other artifacts","SR4U: two weeks, knowledge-acquisition sprint with SMEs filtering manually, MRFs, nine-month roadmap"]],
["b17","How can envisioning be made economically sensible?",["Realistic confidence threshold (avoid the illusion of certainty)","Short horizon, focus on the first release","Act quickly: the market clock is ticking","Pay for validated learning, not for guesses","Incremental/provisional funding; learn fast and pivot"]],
// ---------- Chapter 18: Release Planning ----------
["b18","Compare the release constraint combinations of Table 18.1 and explain which ones Rubin recommends.",["Fixed everything: not recommended; quality becomes flexible","Fixed scope and date: not recommended; game of chicken; adding people late (Brooks)","Fixed scope: date and therefore budget flexible","Fixed date: scope flexible; most aligned with Scrum; works if high-priority items are truly done; small MRFs","Constraints are revisited during the release"]],
["b18","Show how to plan a fixed-date and a fixed-scope release with a velocity range, and how to communicate progress.",["Fixed date: number of sprints x slow and fast velocity -> will-have and might-have lines","Fixed scope: total size / fast and slow velocity, rounded up -> range of sprints","Cost: number of sprints x cost per sprint (or cost per point)","Fixed scope: release burndown or burnup with projections","Fixed date: range of features, burnup with inverted backlog"]],
// ---------- Chapter 19: Sprint Planning ----------
["b19","Describe how you would run a sprint planning session for a team new to Scrum, including how the sprint goal is formed and how the team decides how much to take on.",["Timebox: about four to eight hours for sprints of two weeks to a month","The product owner brings the initial sprint goal and the ordered, ready backlog and answers questions; the ScrumMaster facilitates","Inputs: ready backlog, velocity, constraints, team capabilities, initial goal","Determine capacity, select items (two-part or one-part), break them into tasks of at most about eight hours, check against capacity and velocity","Don't start what you can't finish; refine the goal; the team finalizes its commitment (goal and sprint backlog)"]],
["b19","Explain how a team determines its capacity, in story points and in effort-hours.",["Remove time for other Scrum activities (about a day per two weeks) and grooming (up to 10%)","Remove non-sprint commitments, overhead and personal time off; keep a buffer","Story points: start from average or previous velocity (yesterday's weather), adjust for the sprint","Effort-hours: available days x hours per day per person, as a range (e.g. 140-197)","Check skills capacity, not just total hours"]],
// ---------- Chapter 20: Sprint Execution ----------
["b20","During sprint execution the team discovers that an item is bigger than expected and a stakeholder raises a new urgent request. Describe how the team and product owner should respond while protecting the sprint goal.",["Check the impact on the sprint goal: clarifications are fine, goal-altering changes are not","The bigger item: product owner and team negotiate (split it, drop a lower item); no extended sprint, no lower quality","The new request goes to the product owner, who puts it in the product backlog for a later sprint","A rule, not a law: change the goal only if deferring costs much more than changing","Abnormal termination only if the goal is no longer valid, decided by the product owner"]],
["b20","Describe how a team manages flow during sprint execution.",["Opportunistic task planning, not a full up-front plan (no sprint Gantt)","How many items in parallel: avoid multitasking (row vs column exercise) and underutilization; swarming","Which work to start: next priority, adjusted for dependencies and skills","Organize tasks by value, not roles (testers can start first with test-first); avoid mini waterfall","Who does the work: T-shaped skills; daily scrum for flow; task board, burndown, burnup"]],
// ---------- Chapter 21: Sprint Review ----------
["b21","Explain how a sprint review differs from a status meeting or a sign-off meeting, and how its outcomes feed into planning.",["The review inspects and adapts the product with the stakeholders: it is not a status report","Not a sign-off: the product owner has already reviewed the items during the sprint","Only done, working product is demonstrated; the demo supports the discussion","A stakeholder who disagrees: a new item in the product backlog","Outputs: groomed product backlog and updated release plan, which feed the next sprint planning"]],
["b21","Describe the prework for a sprint review.",["Determine whom to invite: Scrum team, internal stakeholders, other teams, external customers","Schedule it: hardest to schedule; regular cadence; about one hour per sprint week","Confirm that the work is done: product owner reviews during the sprint","Prepare the demo: low ceremony, 30-60 minutes per sprint week","Decide who facilitates (often the ScrumMaster) and who demonstrates"]],
// ---------- Chapter 22: Sprint Retrospective ----------
["b22","Describe how you would facilitate a retrospective so that it avoids blame and its improvements are actually carried out.",["Set the atmosphere: ground rules, safety, focus on the system and the process, not on people","Share context with objective data (event timeline, emotions seismograph), then identify insights at system level","Determine a few concrete actions within capacity, each with an owner, chosen by vote","Close with a recap and appreciation","Follow through: tasks in the sprint backlog, impediments to the ScrumMaster; check the actions at the next retrospective"]],
["b22","Describe the prework and the steps of a sprint retrospective.",["Prework: define the focus, select exercises, gather objective data, structure (time, place, facilitator)","Set the atmosphere: safety, ground rules, everyone speaks","Share context: objective data, event timeline, emotions seismograph","Identify insights: system-level view, silent grouping; determine actions: dot voting, capacity, owners","Close: recap, appreciation, improve the retrospective itself; then follow through"]],
// ---------- Chapter 23: The Path Forward ----------
["b23","Explain why there is no end state for Scrum and how an organization can use Scrum to adopt Scrum.",["No definition of done for adoption, no maturity level 5; agile is something you become more of (Cohn)","Discover your own path: you cannot circumvent your own learning","Practices are core and shared; approaches are team-specific (toss the moose)","Use Scrum to adopt Scrum: Enterprise Transition Community, improvement backlog, sprints","Get going; Scrum exposes dysfunctions but people must solve them"]],
];
